anticanonical line, and the particular diagram supplies
\eqref{har:eq:kernel}; the rank-open condition is not inferred from nefness.

There are absolute constants $L,C_0>0$ such that the following holds. Put $m=\sqrt n$, assume $m/2\le A,D\le2m$, and take $m$ sufficiently large that $\theta=1-L/\log m>0$. For all nonnegative functions $w_i:S_D\to[0,\infty)$ and $v_k:S_A\to[0,\infty)$,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{n!}{|R||C|}\,
 \mathbb E_U\!\left[\mathbf1_{\mathcal I}
       \prod_{i=1}^A w_i(r_i)\prod_{k=1}^D v_k(c_k)\right]
 \le e^{C_0n^{.54}}
       \prod_{i=1}^A(\mathbb E w_i^{1/\theta})^\theta
       \prod_{k=1}^D(\mathbb E v_k^{1/\theta})^\theta .
\end{equation}
The expectations on the right are uniform on the indicated symmetric groups, and logarithms are natural.

There is an absolute finite $p_*>0$ such that, for every dyadic $n$,
\begin{equation}
 Z_n(p_*)\le1+\frac1{16}.
\end{equation}
For every integer $M\ge p_*$, $M$ independent forward sweeps have worst-start total-variation distance at most $1/8$ from uniform.

\textbf{Grid trace estimate.} Suppose at the two child sizes \(A,D\) we have \(Z_{A}(p), Z_{D}(p)\le 2\), \(p\ge 4\). Splitting a sweep into independent sweeps within rows, then independent sweeps within columns, we have
\begin{equation}
        Z_n(t)\ \le\ \exp(O(n^{.54})),\qquad
                  t=p(1+O(1/\log m)),                             
\end{equation}
where \(t\) can be taken as \(p(1+L'/\log m)\) for a suitable absolute \(L'\), for sufficiently large \(m\).

Suppose, for $p>0$ and $0<\varepsilon\le1/2$, that
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{\rho\ne\mathbf1}D_\rho\Tr|B_\rho|^p\le\varepsilon.
\end{equation}
Then every nontrivial $\rho$ and every $1\le r\le D_\rho$ satisfy
\begin{equation}
 s_r(B_\rho)\le\left(\frac{\varepsilon}{D_\rho r}\right)^{1/p}.
\end{equation}
The full regular singular list, with its constant value at index $1$, satisfies
\begin{equation}
 s_j(B)\le j^{-1/p}\qquad(1\le j\le|G|).
\end{equation}
For every integer $M$ with $2M\ge p$, $M$ independent convolution steps have worst-start total-variation distance at most $\tfrac12\sqrt\varepsilon$ from uniform.

Conversely, if $s_j(B)\le j^{-c}$ for an absolute $c>0$ over a family of finite groups and their convolution operators, then for each fixed $\varepsilon>0$ there is a fixed $p'>0$, independent of the group, for which \eqref{e:nonconstant-moment} holds with $p'$ in place of $p$.

There is an absolute $p_*\ge4$ such that
\begin{equation}
              {\rm Tr}|T_n|^{p_*}\ \le\ 1+1/8                         
\end{equation}
for every power of two $n$.

There is a sequence $r(d)\ge2$ with $\sup_d r(d)<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}
\mathcal Z_N(r(d))\le 6/5
\qquad(d\ge1). 
\end{equation}
For every deterministic initial deck and every integer $w\ge\sup_d r(d)$, the law after $wd$ physical shuffles has chi-square divergence at most $1/5$ from uniform, and hence total-variation distance at most $\frac12\sqrt{1/5}$ from uniform.

For all sufficiently large \(d\), if a real \(q\ge16/\beta\) satisfies
\begin{equation}
 M_b(q), M_a(q) \ \le\ 1+c_0,

\end{equation}
then \(p_0=(1+\delta)q\) satisfies
\begin{equation}
 M_n((1+\delta)p_0)\le 1+c_0

\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 M_n(p_0)\le \exp(n^{1-\alpha}).

\end{equation}
The lower threshold for $d$ is absolute and independent of $q$.

There are numbers \(p(d)>0\), with \(\sup_d p(d)<\infty\), such that for every \(n=2^d\),
\begin{equation}
 M_n(p(d))\le 1+c_0,\qquad c_0=\frac18.

\end{equation}

\textbf{Long first row.} For all sufficiently large powers of two \(n\), if
\[
                     1\le k=n-\lambda_1\le n^{.995},
\]
there is a bound
\begin{equation}
               d_\lambda\,{\rm Tr}|T_n(\lambda)|^p\le n^{-10k}
                       \qquad (p\ge p_*)

\end{equation}
with \(p_*<\infty\) absolute.

Arrange the points in \(b\) rows of \(a\) cells each. We need a probability bound, which we prove first. Independently in each row \(i\), assign the labels \(h_{ij}\in[a]\) by a permutation, with an arbitrary law of density at most \(D_i\) relative to uniform. Independently each column \(j\) has labels \(l_{ij}\in[b]\) likewise assigned by a permutation, say with density at most \(D'_j\), also independent across columns and from the row assignments. Put \(D_H=\prod_i D_i,\ D_L=\prod_j D'_j\). Then, with an absolute constant \(C\), the probability that the pairs \((h_{ij},l_{ij})\) are all distinct is at most
\begin{equation}
                \exp\left(-n+C n^{.98}
                        +\frac{C}{\log n}\log(D_H D_L)\right).

\end{equation}

We apply the estimate to products of group projections. Use \(R\) and \(Y\) for the subgroups of \(S_n\) permuting within rows and within columns, respectively. For each row of size \(a\), let \(P_i\) be an orthogonal projection in its group algebra supported just on one irrep \(\mu_i\), of rank \(r_i>0\) there (so acting on the regular representation, this block action is repeated with multiplicity \(d_{\mu_i}\)). Put \(D_i=d_{\mu_i}r_i\). Likewise take projections for the columns in their group algebras with \(D'_j\) defined similarly. Write \(P_R,P_Y\) for the two product projections, viewed also in the group algebra of \(S_n\). On its regular representation,
\begin{equation}
           \|P_Y P_R\|_4^4
                  \le \exp(C n^{.98})\,
                         \left(\prod_i D_i \prod_j D'_j\right)^{1+C/\log n}

\end{equation}
for an absolute \(C\).

We prove that for each sweep size there is \(p_n\ge 4\), all bounded above by an absolute constant, such that
\begin{equation}
                     d_\lambda\,{\rm Tr}|T_n(\lambda)|^{p_n}
                            \ \le\ \exp(n^{.99})
                     \qquad\text{for all }\lambda.

\end{equation}
For all sufficiently large $n$, the proof also bounds the complete regular Schatten power by $\exp(n^{.99})$.

There are absolute \(\alpha>0\), \(M_0<\infty\), and \(0<h<1\), with
\begin{equation}
\|T_n\|_\lambda^2\ \le\ h\left(\frac{M_0 k}{n}\right)^{\alpha k}, \qquad k=n-\lambda_1\ge 1,

\end{equation}
where the norm is operator norm on the indicated representation.

For every probability law $\nu$ supported on valid configurations, with absolute constants and all sufficiently large \(a\),
\begin{equation}
D_{\rm joint}
\ \ge\ n-O(n a^{-1/16})
 +\left(1-\frac{C_3}{\log a}\right)\left(\sum_i D_{A_i}+\sum_c D_{B_c}\right).

\end{equation}

Here is a useful weighted form. Put \(\theta=C_3/\log a\) (increasing the absolute constant if needed), so \(0<\theta<1\) for sufficiently large \(a\). Under uniform independent permutations, for nonnegative functions \(z_i\) of a row permutation and \(w_c\) of a column permutation,
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\log\mathbb E_{\rm unif}\left[\mathbf 1_{\rm valid}
       \prod_i z_i(A_i)^2\prod_c w_c(B_c)^2\right]
\ \le\ &-n+O(n a^{-1/16})\\
 &+2\sum_i\log\|z_i\|_{2/(1-\theta)}
    +2\sum_c\log\|w_c\|_{2/(1-\theta)}
\end{split}

\end{equation}
where the norms are under the marginal uniform probabilities.

Suppose for some \(p\ge2\) we have
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Tr}_{G_a}|T_a|^{2p}\le 2,\qquad
\operatorname{Tr}_{G_b}|T_b|^{2p}\le 2.

\end{equation}
Let \(p'=p(1+\theta)\) with \(\theta\) as in \eqref{tra:projection-deletion:eq16}. We claim
\begin{equation}
\log\operatorname{Tr}_{G_n}|T_n|^{2p'}\ \le\ O(n a^{-1/16}+\sqrt n\log n).

\end{equation}

There are real numbers $p_n\ge2$, indexed by dyadic $n\ge2$, with $\sup_n p_n<\infty$ such that

\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Tr}_{G_n}|T_n|^{2p_n}\ \le\ 1+\tfrac{1}{8}.

\end{equation}

Draw elements \(h\in H\) (column permutations \(h_v,\ 1\le v\le b\)) and \(j\in J_0\) (row permutations \(j_r,\ 1\le r\le a\)) with all permutations independent, from possibly nonuniform laws. Suppose on each factor the pointwise density versus uniform is bounded, with the product of those upper bounds \(e^z\). Then
\begin{equation}
              \Pr\{ h j\in J_0 H\}
                  \le \exp\{-n+C n^{.98} + C z/\log n\}.
\end{equation}
Here \(h j\) means apply \(j\) and then \(h\). The coefficient of $-n$ is essential: the later regular-trace
normalization contributes $+n$ to leading order.

There is an absolute $a>0$ such that


\begin{equation}
 s_r(T_n\text{ in }\lambda)\ \le\ (D_\lambda r)^{-a},
 \qquad 1\le r\le D_\lambda,\quad n\text{ a power of }2,             
\end{equation}
with singular values ordered largest first. The claim as written allows 1 on a one-dimensional representation; on the nontrivial sign we can in fact use 0.

For the balanced sizes \(m,q\) here, let \(L=\log n\), and for sufficiently large \(n\) write \(\epsilon=L^{-1/5}\). We claim
\begin{equation}
       \|P_H P_J\|_{4,\mathrm{reg}(G)}^4
              \le (R_H R_J)^{1+\epsilon}
                       \exp\{ n\exp(-L^{1/2})\}.                            
\end{equation}
This estimate in particular bounds the Schatten norm within shape \(\lambda\), with the fourth power on the right divided by \(D_\lambda\), by the multiplicity in the regular representation.

For every \(\lambda\vdash n\) with deficit \(k=n-\lambda_1\ge 1\),
\begin{equation}
              \|P_H P_J\|_{4,\lambda}^4
                    \le \exp(C k)\,\frac{R_H R_J}{D_\lambda}.             
\end{equation}

There is an absolute $c>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
                             s_j(T_N)\le j^{-c}\qquad (1\le j\le N!)
\end{equation}
for every power of two $N$.

The following estimate holds for sufficiently large \(n\) factored as \(n=l m\) with \(1/2\le l/m\le 2\). Let \(G\) be the permutation group of \([l]\times[m]\), with \(H\) its subgroup of permutations acting separately in each row and \(K\) the subgroup acting separately in each column. For \(h\in H,\ k\in K\), consider the event
\begin{equation}
hk\in KH. 
\end{equation}
Suppose \(h,k\) are chosen with independent component permutations (all \(l+m\) permutations independent). Use \(L_v\) as bounds on the supremum densities, relative to uniform, for the individual permutation laws, indexing these components by \(v\). Then for an absolute \(C\),
\begin{equation}
\mathbb P\{hk\in KH\}
\ \le\
\exp\left(-n+C n^{0.55}+\frac{C}{\log n}\sum_v\log L_v\right). 
\end{equation}

There are uniformly bounded real exponents \(q_n\ge 300\), for the powers of two \(n\ge 2\), such that
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Tr} \mathcal Q_n^{q_n}\ \le\ 1+\tfrac18. 
\end{equation}
An absolute integer number of sweeps therefore has chi-square divergence at most $1/8$.

If a tensor of cost \(e^C\) has a readable label tree, then
\begin{equation}
 W(r_{XY}(\pi),r_{XZ}(\pi),r_{YZ}(\pi))\le C
\end{equation}
for every leaf law \(\pi\).

For \(U=F\) or \(U=F_p\), and \(0\le k\le1\),
the largest feasible scale on this ray is
\begin{equation}
 d_U(k):=\max\{d\ge0:(2d,kd)\in\calH_U\}
       =\min_{0\le t\le1}\frac{U(t)}{2+kt}.
\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation}
 w(k)\le\frac1{d_U(k)}
      =\max_{0\le t\le1}\frac{2+kt}{U(t)}.
\end{equation}
A maximizing parameter is the leftmost \(t\in[0,1]\) for which
\begin{equation}
 (2+kt)U'_+(t)\ge kU(t),
\end{equation}
or \(t=1\) if no such point exists.

The sequence $f_n/n$ converges to a real number $\alpha$. For every integer $k\ge1$,
\begin{equation}
 \alpha\ge\frac{f_k}{k}-2^{67}k^{-1/6}.
\end{equation}

For all integers $n\ge1$ and $0\le r\le n$,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{\E F_r}{n}\le\frac{f_n}{n}
       +C_4(r/n)^{1/6},\qquad C_4=2^{53}.
\end{equation}
For every fixed real $a>\alpha$, there exists $\epsilon>0$ such that, with probability
tending to one, the relaxed formula at time $an$ cannot be made satisfiable
by deleting $\lfloor\epsilon n\rfloor$ variables. On the same event every
assignment violates more than $\lfloor\epsilon n\rfloor$ clauses.

For every fixed real $0\le a<\alpha$ and every fixed $\beta>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac1n\E\log Z_n(a,\beta)\ge0.
\end{equation}
For every fixed real $a>\alpha$, some positive integer $\beta$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \limsup_{n\to\infty}\frac1n\E\log Z_n(a,\beta)<0.
\end{equation}

For every $a>0$ and finite $\beta>0$, each finite-depth strict site-only
trial satisfies
\begin{equation}
 L_n\le nG_1\quad(n\ge1),\qquad P(a,\beta)\le G_1.

\end{equation}

For all sufficiently large $d$, let $T=2048d$ and let $\mu$ be its
permutation law. There is a
partition $D_1,\ldots,D_8$ of the starting positions into sets of size
$n/8$ and a subprobability $0\le f\le\mu$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \sum_g f(g)\ge1-8n^{-11},\qquad
 f(gH_i)\le2\frac{|H_i|}{|G|}
 \quad(g\in G,\ 1\le i\le8),

\end{equation}
where $H_i=\Sym(D_i)$ fixes the complement pointwise.

For the Thorp shuffle on $n=2^d$, each of the following methods proves
worst-start total-variation convergence to uniform at the indicated time:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the sixteen-subgroup orbit-span argument at $8192d$;
\item predictable coset caps at $16384d$;
\item diagram peeling at $400\cdot2^{22}d$;
\item the covariance and four-subgroup coefficient argument at $16000d$.
\end{enumerate}
The $2048d$ fixed-list and isotypic route was completed in
Section~\ref{sec:first-route}.

For $|F|\ge n/8$ and $\sum_xf(x)=0$, the signed-weight process satisfies,
for all sufficiently large $d$,
\begin{equation}
 \E\|f_{2d}\|_2^2\le n^{-1/256}\|f_0\|_2^2.
\end{equation}
Consequently $4096$ sweeps mix every fixed list of at most $7n/8$ cards
with variation error at most $\frac12 n^{-5/2}$.

Fix $p\in(0,1)$ and set $r=\lfloor d/2\rfloor$. For arbitrary
$|F|\ge pn$ and zero-sum $f$, two sweeps satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \E\|f_{2d}\|_2^2\le
 \left((1-p/4)^{d-r}+2^{-r}
             +2^{d-r}e^{-p2^r/8}\right)\|f_0\|_2^2.
\end{equation}
In particular the factor is at most $n^{-\gamma_p}$ eventually, where
$\gamma_p=-\frac14\log_2(1-p/4)>0$.
It is also at most $n^{-p/16}$ eventually.

For $t\ge d$,
\begin{equation}
 A_t=\sum_{\ell=1}^d2^{-\ell}S_{t-\ell}.

\end{equation}
There are constants $c_\alpha,C_\alpha>0$, depending only on $\alpha$,
such that, with $\gamma=\alpha/2$ and
$b_n=C_\alpha n e^{-c_\alpha n}$,
\begin{equation}
 S_s\le(1-\gamma)A_s+b_n\qquad(s\ge d-1).

\end{equation}
In particular, for $\theta=1-\gamma/4$,
\begin{equation}
 A_t\le n\theta^{t-2d}+b_n/\gamma\qquad(t\ge0).

\end{equation}
Consequently there is a fixed integer $C>2$, depending only on $\alpha$,
for which $A_{Cd}=O(n^{-4})$, uniformly in $m\in[\alpha n,n]$.

For all sufficiently large $d$,
\begin{equation}
 \|\widehat K(\lambda)\|_{\rm op}\le n^{-k/10}
 \quad\hbox{if}\quad 1\le k=n-\lambda_1\le n^{1/20}.

\end{equation}
If $\lambda$ has more than $n/2$ rows, then
$\widehat K(\lambda)=0$.

Fix $C_0<\infty$. If $\lambda\vdash N=2^d$ has first-row length
$N-k$, where $1\le k\le C_0d^{16}$, then, for all sufficiently large
$d$ depending only on $C_0$, the law $\kappa$ of one sweep satisfies
\begin{equation}
\|\widehat\kappa(\lambda)\|_{\mathrm{HS}}\le N^{-k/10}.                
\end{equation}

Let $\psi:D\to\H$ send $a,c,d$ to $0,1,\infty$, and put
$x=\psi(b)\in(0,1)$.  Under the boundary and approximation conventions
above,
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{n\to\infty}\mathbb P(C_n)
 =\frac{\displaystyle\int_0^x[t(1-t)]^{-2/3}\,\dd t}
        {\displaystyle\int_0^1[t(1-t)]^{-2/3}\,\dd t}.
\end{equation}

With the definitions above,
\begin{align}
 2P+2D_*+4(C_0+C_1)&\le o(\log n),\\
 C_0+C_1+L_0+L_1&\le o(\log n),\\
 P+\sum_j\log\frac{F_-(a_j)}{F_+(a_j)}
   &\le o(\log n),
\end{align}
where the finite list of fixed charges in the last inequality satisfies
\eqref{exp:charge-range} and $\sum_j a_j=1$.

On every compact capacity interval, the Dobrushin driving functions
converge in law, uniformly, to $\sqrt\kappa B_t$, where $B$ is standard
Brownian motion and
\begin{equation}
 \kappa=\frac{8}{1+h}
        =\frac{4\pi}{\arccos(-\sqrt q/2)}.
\end{equation}

In its own radial clock, the limiting gap is the unique instantaneous
reflected diffusion on $[0,2\pi]$ with interior generator
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal A f(\theta)=\frac\kappa2 f''(\theta)
       +\frac{\kappa-4}{2}\cot(\theta/2)f'(\theta).
\end{equation}
The forcepoint has no singular push:
\begin{equation}
 O_t=O_0+\int_0^t\cot(\theta_s/2)\,ds.
\end{equation}
Equation~\eqref{tree:sde} consequently holds globally, including
collisions, with locally integrable drift.

For every even $k\ge2$ and every compact $K\subset\mathcal D_k$,
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{\delta\downarrow0}
 \sup_{\boldsymbol u\in K}\sup_{I\in\operatorname{Isom}(\R^2)}
 \left|\Phi_{k,\delta}(\boldsymbol u)
             -\Phi_{k,\delta}(I\boldsymbol u)\right|=0.

\end{equation}

For every real $\rho\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb C)$ with $\int\rho=0$,
the independent fair unit-jump height of critical square-lattice
$\FK_4$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{\delta\downarrow0}
 \mathbb E\exp\bigl(i\langle h_\delta,\rho\rangle\bigr)
 =\exp\left\{-\frac{\sigma^2}{2}
  \iint\rho(x)G_{\mathbb C}(x,y)\rho(y)\,dx\,dy\right\}.
\end{equation}
The same assertion holds jointly for finitely many such tests.

Let $(E,h)$ be a connected complete $n$-dimensional exterior, including
its compact frontier $\Sigma$ in the enclosing-set sense above. Suppose
that $h$ is smooth on $E$ and through $\Sigma$, and that
\begin{enumerate}
\item $R_h\ge0$, $R_h\in L^1(E,dV_h)$, and
      $R_h=O(r^{-n-\beta})$ for some $\beta>0$ on the end;
\item outside a compact set there is exactly one Euclidean coordinate
      end with $h-\delta=O_2(r^{-\tau})$, where $\tau>d/2$;
\item the full frontier is outer minimizing and locally perimeter
      minimizing as the boundary of the filled side. Its regular part
      is a smooth embedded minimal hypersurface, and its singular set
      has Hausdorff dimension at most $n-8$.
\end{enumerate}
Then
\begin{equation}
 m_{\rm ADM}(h)\ge\frac12
 \left(\frac{\mathcal H_h^k(\Sigma)}{\omega}\right)^{d/k}.
\end{equation}

Under the common law of $(\mathcal D,e)$, for every $i\in[M]$,
\begin{equation}
  \E h_u(p_i)\ge\frac{p_i}{4}.

\end{equation}

For every \(i\in[M]\), under the common distribution of
\((\mathcal D,e)\),
\begin{equation}

  \E\bigl[h_u(p_i)(1-h_v(q_i))\bigr]\le\frac{p_i}{16}.
\end{equation}

For fixed \(m\ge2\), \(M<\infty\), and \(r_0=m+15\), there are
\(K,N\), independent of \(0\le\sigma\le1\), such that for
\(\|f\|_{r_0,\sigma}\le M\) and \(|n|\ge N\),
\begin{equation}
 (\dd C_n)_f(v)=[b_n(f)v]_n,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{align}
 \|b_n(f)-1\|_{m,(1-K/n^2)\sigma}&\le K/|n|,
 \\
 \|b_n(f)-b_n(\widehat f)\|_{m,(1-K/n^2)\sigma}
 &\le (K/|n|)\|f-\widehat f\|_{r_0,\sigma}.

\end{align}
The second estimate holds for two inputs with the same bound.
The derivative identity holds for smooth real variations and for
real-symmetric holomorphic variations with the indicated finite norms.

The construction above is deterministic and polynomial time.  With
\begin{equation}
 C_s=C_0+\sum_{\{i,j\}\in\mathcal C}K_{ij}
     =3k\Delta+\frac32\sum_e v_e^2
                        +\sum_{\{i,j\}\in\mathcal C}K_{ij},
\end{equation}
it satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \left|E\bigl(H_{\rm Hub}(t)\bigr)
            -\alpha\bigl(E(H_{\rm in})-C_s\bigr)\right|
        \leq\frac{3\alpha\gamma}{64}.
\end{equation}
The scalar $C_s$ has polynomial magnitude and can be approximated to
any polynomial-bit absolute precision in polynomial time.

At any fixed rational centers satisfying the hypotheses of this
section, and with the integer charges in \eqref{eq:wells-charges}
already fixed, one can compute rational
\(\rho_1,\ldots,\rho_m\in[1,4]\) such that
\begin{equation}
 |\lambda_i+1/2|\le e^{-1.01D}\qquad(1\le i\le m).

\end{equation}
The algorithm is deterministic and polynomial in \(D,m\) and the
input bit lengths. Every parameter it samples or outputs has
\(O(D)\) fractional bits.

Suppose the actual rounded charges and the parameters $\rho_i$ obey
the conclusions of Proposition~\ref{prop:tuning}, in particular
$|\lambda_i+1/2|\le e^{-1.01D}$.  Uniformly for $r_i\le2D$,
\begin{equation}
 \psi_i(y)=\pi^{-1/2}e^{-r_i+F_i(y)}
       \bigl(1+O(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{rel}})\bigr).

\end{equation}

Let \(E_0=\inf\operatorname{Spec}H\) for the physical molecular
Hamiltonian and let \(E_{\mathrm{Hub}}(t)\) be the minimum of
the half-filled Hubbard Hamiltonian in
Proposition~\ref{prop:hubbard}.  The construction satisfies
\begin{align}
 \left|\frac{E_0}{Z_*}+\frac{mZ_*}{2}
                   -E_{\mathrm{Hub}}(t)\right|
 &\le m\delta_1+\frac{Cm^5}{D}+Z_*e^{-1.2D}
       \\
 &\le C\biggl[
       m^2\epsilon_{\mathrm{hop}}+\frac{m^5}{D}
       +m e^{-.01D}+m^2e^{-.1D}\notag\\
 &\hspace{35mm}{}+m^2e^{-.6D}+e^{-.2D}
       \biggr].
\end{align}
In particular, for every prescribed fixed inverse-polynomial
error budget \(\epsilon_*\), the polynomial scale and calibration
precision can be chosen so that the left side is at most
\(\epsilon_*\).

For all sufficiently large $\Delta$ depending only on $r$, suppose that
a proper partial correspondence coloring and entry weights satisfy
availability, \eqref{eq:weight-bounds}, and
\begin{equation}
 K/2\le W_v\le2K\qquad(v\in V(G')).
\end{equation}
There is an extension of the partial coloring and a choice of weights
on the remaining entries that preserve availability and
\eqref{eq:weight-bounds}, and satisfy
\begin{equation}
 W_v^{\mathrm{new}}\le W_v+\xi,\qquad
 F_v^{\mathrm{new}}\le F_v+\xi,\qquad
 \frac{\deg_{\mathrm{new}}(v)}{\Delta}
 \le(1-\tau)\frac{\deg_{G'}(v)}{\Delta}+\xi
\end{equation}
for every vertex $v$ that remains uncolored.

Projective dimensions in the Ext sense satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{pd}_{\mathcal N}(\mathcal O)\leq4,\qquad
 \operatorname{pd}_{\mathcal M_k}(\mathcal O),
 \operatorname{pd}_{\mathcal M_0}(\mathcal O)\leq5,\qquad
 \operatorname{pd}_{\mathcal M}(\mathcal O)\leq6.
\end{equation}
For sheaves on $\mathcal M_k$, the right adjoint of $r^*$ has
\begin{equation}
 r_*M=\ker(c-1:M\to M),\qquad
 R^1r_*M=\coker(c-1:M\to M),\qquad R^{>1}r_*M=0,
\end{equation}
where the kernel and cokernel descend to $\mathcal N$.

If $Y=\mathcal R(\mathsf P)$ for a perfect right dg
$\mathscr E$-module $\mathsf P$, then naturally
\begin{equation}

 \begin{split}
 \pi_*Y&=H_*\bigl(\mathsf P\otimes^{\mathbf L}_{\mathscr E}
                           \mathsf L\bigr),\\
 W(Y)&=H_*\bigl(\mathsf P\otimes^{\mathbf L}_{\mathscr E}
                           \Phi[t^{\pm1}]\bigr).
 \end{split}
\end{equation}
For a finite-cell model the ordinary cellular tensor computes each
derived tensor. The actions in these formulas are the left actions
of \eqref{complete:action-on-sections}.

Under the hypotheses above, there is a finite list $\mathcal A$ of
real-valued functions on $X$, of cardinality $Q$, such that every
$f\in\mathcal F_+$ has an approximant $A\in\mathcal A$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
  f(x)-3\theta\le A(x)\le f(x)+\theta\qquad(x\in X).

\end{equation}
In particular, $\|f-A\|_{\infty,X}\le3\theta$.  The list can be chosen with
\begin{equation}
  \log Q\le hs\log q+C_{h,\theta,u},
  \qquad
  C_{h,\theta,u}
   =\log u+hs2^u\log\left(1+\frac{s2^u}{\theta}\right).

\end{equation}

Let $0<\theta<1$ and let $r,h$ be positive integers.  Put
\begin{equation}
  w=2D_h,\qquad
  u=\left\lceil\frac{hw}{\theta}\right\rceil,

\end{equation}
and let $p$ be any prime satisfying
\begin{equation}
  p>\max\{h,h^2u^{2h}\}.

\end{equation}
There exist $t_1,\ldots,t_u\in E=\F_p^r$ such that, for every nonzero
$Z\in\operatorname{Sym}^h(E^*)$, there is a set $T\subseteq[u]$ with
$|[u]\setminus T|\le\theta u$ for which
\begin{equation}
  Z(t_{i_1},\ldots,t_{i_h})\ne0
  \qquad\text{for all }(i_1,\ldots,i_h)\in T^h.

\end{equation}
Tuples in this assertion may have repeated indices.

Let $a=p$ with $p\geq5$, or $a=p+1$ with $p\geq7$. Then
\begin{equation}
 F^s\pi_*B_a=0\qquad(s\geq a^2+2).
\end{equation}

For all sufficiently large $j$, the exact maps from entry scale $i$
to stop $h$ can be chosen with the following properties.
\begin{enumerate}
\item At the stop, in the fixed norm regimes for the ordinary and
defect activities,
\begin{equation}
 \max_{Q\in\{M,LM\}}
 \bigl(\norm{K^0_{h,Q}}+\norm{K^l_{h,Q}}\bigr)\le \frac{C}{j}.
\end{equation}
The bulk copies satisfy the sharper conclusion of
Proposition~\ref{prop:bulk-window}.  On shift-good supports, pierced
activities are exactly ordinary activities evaluated at the local
lift.  Interior pierced activities copy the plane calculation at
the translate $\gamma_Q$, whenever their required clearance holds.

\item Let $a^\nu_{k,Q,b}$ be the designated scalar coefficient at
coarse block $b$ in step $k$, for $\nu=0,l$, and set
\begin{equation}
 P^\nu_Q=\prod_{k=i}^{h-1}\prod_b(1+a^\nu_{k,Q,b}).
\end{equation}
All factors are real and positive.  The ratios agree exactly:
\begin{equation}
 \frac{P^l_M}{P^0_M}=\frac{P^l_{LM}}{P^0_{LM}}=:\rho_j.
\end{equation}
For every fixed $\epsilon_2>0$, the initial smallness choices can
be made so that $|\log\rho_j|\le\epsilon_2j$ for all sufficiently
large $j$.

\item There are a real scalar $\sigma_Q$ and residual insertion
activities $I_{h,Q}$ such that integration of the entry insertion
over all shells below $v$ gives the exact field identity
\begin{equation}
 \E_{i:h}\mathscr I_i(K^l_{i,Q},I_{i,Q};\phi)
 =P^l_Q\left\{
    \sigma_Q\mathscr Z_h(K^l_{h,Q};\phi)
       +\mathscr I_h(K^l_{h,Q},I_{h,Q};\phi)\right\}.
\end{equation}
Here $\E_{i:h}$ denotes successive shell integration, with the
remaining field $\phi$ held fixed.  The scalar is common to the
two squares,
\begin{equation}
 \sigma_M=\sigma_{LM}=: \sigma_j,
\end{equation}
and every support of $I_{h,Q}$ contains the designated mark block.
Moreover,
\begin{equation}
 \norm{I_{h,Q}}\le C_Rs^{C_R}L^{-2(h-i)/3},
 \qquad Q\in\{M,LM\}.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
The products in \eqref{eq:mag-background-products} contain only
the RG extraction factors.  They do not include the common entry
scalar or the harmonic-energy factor of the Gaussian sector law.

For $n_j=L^j/2$, there exists $\epsilon>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \log a_{n_{j+1}}-\log a_{n_j}
   =-\frac{\log L}{8}+\frac1{16j}
       +O_L(j^{-1-\epsilon}).

\end{equation}

There is $C<\infty$ such that, for every integer $k\ge1$,
\begin{equation}
 \sup_w\Pr(S_k=w)\le Ck^{-8/3},\qquad
 \sum_{m\in\mathbb Z}\sup_{H(w)=m}\Pr(S_k=w)
 \le Ck^{-4/3}.
\end{equation}

For every $\eps>0$ there exists $C_\eps<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \norm{Uf}_{L^3(\R^3\times[1,2])}
 \le C_\eps\norm{J^\eps f}_{L^3(\R^3)}
 \qquad (f\in\mathcal S(\R^3)).
\end{equation}

Let $L\geq 2$, and let the Fourier transform of $F$ be supported in an
$O(L^{-1})$-neighborhood of a fixed compact annular patch of the cone in
$\R^3$. Use a smooth, boundedly overlapping decomposition into angular
sectors $\vartheta$ of width $L^{-1/2}$. For each dyadic
$L^{-1/2}\lesssim R\lesssim 1$, let $\tau$ range over a boundedly
overlapping cover by angular caps of width $R$. Let $U_{\tau,L}$ be the
associated envelope box, with dimensions $L$, $LR$, and $LR^2$ in the
adapted ray frame, and tile space by its translates. Then, for every
$\eps>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \norm{F}_{L^4}^4
 \lesssim_{\eps} L^{\eps}
 \sum_{L^{-1/2}\lesssim R\lesssim 1}
 \sum_{\tau:\,|\tau|\sim R}
 \sum_{U\parallel U_{\tau,L}} |U|^{-1}
 \left(\int_U\sum_{\vartheta\subset O(\tau)}
                    \norm{F_\vartheta}^2\right)^2.
 \tag{GWZ}
\end{equation}
The assertion holds for functions taking values in a separable Hilbert
space, with a constant independent of that space. Fixed thickness
constants, bounded enlargements of tiles, and adapted rapidly decreasing
tile weights are allowed.

Suppose the root analyzed measure has mass $e$ and satisfies
\[
 \mu_0\{|N-n|\leq f,\ |D-b|\leq f\}\leq b_0 f^{1-\eta}
 \qquad(f>0,\ f\geq d_1).
\]
Here the subscript $1$ means time length one. At all terminal intervals
of length $\tau$, make disjoint fractional assignments to tests of normal
velocity width $f_\lambda$ and current-position width $\tau f_\lambda$,
where $f_\lambda\geq d_\tau$. The centers are constant on each test;
no shear depending on an exact index is used in these tests. With any
fixed finite number of admissible masks,
\begin{equation}
 \sum_\lambda \frac{m_\lambda^2}{f_\lambda^{1-\eta}}
 \leq \tau^{-\eta}s^{-o(1)}b_0e.
 \tag{SC}
\end{equation}
This holds along polynomial sequences for which $\tau^{-1}$,
$b_0/e,e/b_0$, the phase density and support bounds, and $H,H^{-1}$
when applicable are bounded by fixed powers of $s^{-1}$.

At almost every interior admissible root there are arbitrarily
small tangent gaps with sufficiently accurate preceding
experiments such that, for any fixed finite collection of
queries,
\begin{equation}
 I_\delta(F_{\leq p'};\boldsymbol\Xi\mid C_*)=o(1).
 \tag{vb}
\end{equation}
Define a fiber read \(Q_i\), at a sufficiently large finite
depth relative to still finer stored marks, by binning
\[
 B+\theta_iX,\quad Y+\theta_iX^2,\quad D_0'+\theta_iN,
 \quad
 \begin{cases}
 P-2r_iX,\ N-PX+r_iX^2,&\text{quadratic},\\
 N-r_iX,&\text{linear}.
 \end{cases}
\]
Then
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 I_\delta(X_{\leq p'};Q_i,\boldsymbol\Xi\mid C_*)&=o(1),\\
 i_\delta(X_{\leq p'}\mid C_*)&=m(a,v)p'+o(1)
                         \quad\text{in probability}.
 \end{split}
 \tag{fib}
\end{equation}
At a fixed boundary root with one observation, only the
consequence of \eqref{eq:ng} for adding that observation and
its fiber read is asserted; neither \eqref{eq:vb} nor an
interior angular trace is asserted there.

For admissible interior tangent data,
\begin{equation}
 \partial_a\mathcal H\geq d+
 \min(1,c)\bigl(\bar w+\overline{|x_i|}\bigr)
 +|1-c|
 \begin{cases}
  \overline{|\pi_i|},&c<1,\\
  \overline{|G_i|},&c>1,
 \end{cases}
 \tag{time-rate}
\end{equation}
where the last term is omitted at $c=1$. In the linear case,
$\partial_v\mathcal H\leq\bar\alpha+1$.

In the quadratic case define, for $p\leq z\leq2p$, the Heisenberg
name $F_{p,z}$ by reading $X,B,P$ to depth $p$ and
\[
 Y-2[X]_pB,\qquad N-[P]_pX,\qquad D-[P]_pB
\]
to depth $z$. Let
\begin{equation}
 e(\lambda)=\frac{H_\delta(F_{2\lambda}\mid\mathcal D)
                -H_\delta(F_{\lambda,2\lambda}\mid\mathcal D)}{\lambda}
\end{equation}
in the limiting entropy law. Then
\begin{equation}
 \partial_v\mathcal H=2\bar\alpha-\bar e.
 \tag{width-rate}
\end{equation}

Put $l_* = \min(1,d+m)$. For quadratic data with $m>0$ and
radial spreading, write $C_z=(H,J,K)$. Then
\begin{equation}
 |W_i\mid C_z|\geq l_*,\qquad
 |C_z|-|x_i|\geq m+l_*,\qquad
 \alpha-|C_z|\geq2m+l_*.
 \tag{modes}
\end{equation}
When the quadratic $G_i$ is used and line avoidance holds,
\begin{equation}
 |G_i|\geq2m+|x_i|,\qquad
 2\E|G_i|\geq\E\alpha-1.
 \tag{Gq}
\end{equation}
For linear data with $m>0$ and crossing,
\begin{equation}
 |W_i\mid H|\geq l_*,\qquad |x_i|\geq l_*,\qquad
 |G_i|\geq m+l_*,\qquad
 2\E|G_i|\geq\E\alpha-1.
 \tag{modes-lin}
\end{equation}
For linear data, without a positivity assumption on $m$,
\begin{equation}
 w-|\pi_i|\leq1.
 \tag{miss}
\end{equation}

For quadratic data with $m>0$ and radial spreading,
\begin{equation}
 |x_i|\geq d.
 \tag{cross-q}
\end{equation}
If also $c<1$, set $a_*=(d-c\nu)_+$ and
$n_* =\min(1,d+\nu)$. Then
\begin{equation}
 |x_i|\geq n_*,\qquad |H\mid x_i|\geq a_*,\qquad
 |C_z|-|x_i|\geq a_*+\nu,\qquad
 |\pi_i|\geq l_*+a_*+|x_i|.
 \tag{mix-q}
\end{equation}
For linear data with crossing, $m>0$, $c<1$, and $\nu>0$,
\begin{equation}
 |x_i|\geq n_*,\qquad |\pi_i|\geq2l_*+n_*,\qquad
 w-|\pi_i|\geq\nu.
 \tag{mix-l}
\end{equation}
The many-leg term in this assertion is obtained with
an arbitrary strict small loss using finitely many replicas.

For linear admissible data with $m>0$ and crossing,
\begin{equation}
 \begin{array}{ll}
 |\pi_i|\geq2d+l_* ,&c<1,\ \nu=0,\\
 w-|x_i|\geq2d,&c=1,\\
 w-|G_i|\geq2d,&c>1.
 \end{array}
 \tag{double}
\end{equation}

For quadratic admissible tangent data,
$\bar e\geq2m$. If $m>0$ and radial spreading holds,
\begin{equation}
 \bar e\geq2m+d.
 \tag{e-q}
\end{equation}

In the wave contradiction, a homogeneous saturating family at any
positive-depth cut $j$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \frac{r_j}{d_{\ell_j}}=s^{o(1)}.
 \tag{round-Pl}
\end{equation}
After normalizing the terminal angular width to $r_1=1$, it follows that
\begin{equation}
 H=s^{1+o(1)},\qquad u(a)=\frac{1-a}{2},\qquad
 r_a=s^{u(a)+o(1)}.
 \tag{round-u}
\end{equation}

There is a universal constant $C$ with the following properties.
Let $\lambda>0$ be sufficiently small relative to $\eps$.
If
$|\alpha|^2-4\beta d_0\lesssim\rho^{2\lambda}$, one may choose
$0<h'<\lambda h$, inside any prescribed available positive gap, so
that
\begin{equation}
 \Delta^{-1/2}\sum_\lambda m_\lambda^{3/2}
 \le s^{-o(1)}\sqrt{\kappa_*}\,e_g.
 \tag{tiny-cap}
\end{equation}
In the complementary case choose a fixed
$0<h'\le\eps h/20$ inside the available gap, then take
$\lambda$ sufficiently small relative to $h'/h$.  With
$P=\rho^{-\lambda}$,
\begin{equation}
 \Delta^{-1/2}\sum_\lambda m_\lambda^{3/2}
 \lesssim s^{-o(1)}P^C\Delta^{-4\eta}
                    \sqrt{\kappa_*}\,e_g.
 \tag{sec}
\end{equation}
The estimates hold classically even after further positive
restrictions or raywise contractions.  For waves they concern the free
continuation of the specified input state.

Passing to finite-list subsequences and then a diagonal, there are
Lipschitz profiles
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 D(a,v)&=\lim\left(i_s(C(a,v))-
                              \log_{1/s}(e/\kappa)\right),\\
 J(a,v,w)&=\lim i_s(A_w\mid C(a,v)),\qquad v\le w\le u(a),
 \end{split}
 \tag{prof}
\end{equation}
in probability on each of the valid measuring laws.  For classical
rays only $D(a,0)$ and $J(a,0,w)$, $0\le w\le1$, are used.
The wave excess satisfies
\begin{equation}
 E(a,v):=D(a,v)-4v-(p+d)a\ge0,
 \qquad E(a,u(a))=0.
 \tag{excess}
\end{equation}
Classically $D(a,0)\ge(p+d)a$ and $D(1,0)\le p+d$.
Furthermore $0\le J(a,v,w)\le2(w-v)$, $J$ is nonincreasing in
$a$ for fixed $v,w$, and in the wave case
\begin{equation}
 J(a,v,w)\ge J(a,v,x)+J(a,x,w),\qquad v<x<w.
\end{equation}
Every bounded-branching score increment in these assertions has the
same limit in expectation as in probability.

Use the exponent data $d,p,\Gamma$ of Equation~\eqref{eq:rt-pd}.
For almost every classical depth $a\in(0,1)$ at which
$D(a,0)$ is differentiable and Lemma~\ref{lem:rt-stationarity}
applies, put $o_T=\partial_aD(a,0)$ and $m=3-p$.
For waves, let $(a,v)$ be almost every interior point with
$0<a<1$ and $0<v<(1-a)/2$ at which $D$ is differentiable and
Lemmas~\ref{lem:rt-interval-density} and \ref{lem:rt-stationarity}
apply, and put $o_T=\partial_aD(a,v)$ and $m=m(a,v)$.
In either case,
\begin{equation}
 o_T\ge4d+\min(1-d,m/2).
 \tag{round-out}
\end{equation}

For every $\delta>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \norm{W_Hg}_{L^3(\R^3\times[1,2])}
 \le C_\delta H^{-\delta}\norm{g}_{L^3(\R^3)}.

\end{equation}

For every $2<p<\infty$ and
$\alpha>\max\{0,1-3/p\}$,
\begin{equation}
 \norm{Uf}_{L^p(\R^3\times[1,2])}
 \le C_{p,\alpha}\norm{J^\alpha f}_{L^p(\R^3)}
 \qquad(f\in\mathcal S(\R^3)).

\end{equation}

Let $3\le p<\infty$ and $s>1-2/p$. Then
\begin{equation}
 \left\|\sup_{0<t<1}|Uf(\cdot,t)|\right\|_{L^p(\R^3)}
 \le C_{p,s}\norm{J^s f}_{L^p(\R^3)}
 \qquad(f\in\mathcal S(\R^3)).
\end{equation}
The family has a bounded maximal extension to $W^{s,p}(\R^3)$, and
in that extension every $f\in W^{s,p}(\R^3)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{t\downarrow0}Uf(x,t)=f(x)
 \quad\text{for almost every }x\in\R^3.
\end{equation}

Let $3\le p<\infty$ and $\delta>1-3/p$. Then
\begin{equation}
 \norm{B_*^\delta f}_{L^p(\R^3)}
 \le C_{p,\delta}\norm f_{L^p(\R^3)}
 \qquad(f\in\mathcal S(\R^3)).
\end{equation}
The family has a bounded maximal extension to $L^p(\R^3)$, and in that
extension every $f\in L^p(\R^3)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{R\to\infty}B_R^\delta f(x)=f(x)
 \quad\text{for almost every }x\in\R^3.
\end{equation}
In particular, every $\delta>0$ is allowed when $p=3$.

For every sufficiently small fixed \(\epsilon>0\), there are
\(s_0,a>0\) and \(K_{\log}<\infty\) such that, at every deterministic
time in the indicated range,
\begin{equation}
 \|\Sigma_s\|\le\frac{1+\epsilon}{s},
 \qquad 0<s\le s_0,\qquad ns^{3/2}\ge K_{\log}\log n,

\end{equation}
outside an integrated planted exceptional set, intersected with
\(\mathcal G\), of mass at most \(e^{-ans^{3/2}}\).
For fixed \(1/2<\gamma<\gamma'<1\), the same exceptional set may be
used for
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 \|A_0\Sigma_sA_0\|&\le r^{-\gamma},\qquad A_0=2I-J,\\
 v_s&:=\sup_{\substack{H^T=-H\\ \|H\|_{\mathrm{HS}}\le1}}
 \operatorname{Var}_{\mu_{h_s}}
 \bigl((X-m_s^{\mathrm{post}})^T[H,J](X-m_s^{\mathrm{post}})\bigr)
       \le C_v s^{-1-\gamma'/2}.
 \end{split}

\end{equation}

For every fixed $\omega>0$ there are $c_\omega>0$ and disorder events of
probability tending to one on which, simultaneously for every nonzero real
function $f$ with $p=\mu_J(f\ne0)\le1/2$,
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal D_J(f)
   \ge c_\omega n^{-2/3-\omega}\log(1/p)\,\mu_J(f^2).

\end{equation}
In particular the spectral gap for rate-one-per-site heat-bath dynamics is
at least $c_\omega(\log2)n^{-2/3-\omega}$ on the same event.

For every fixed $\epsilon>0$,
\begin{align}
 \Prob\!\left\{n^{2/3-\epsilon}\le t_{\rm mix}
                   \le e^{\epsilon n}\right\}&\longrightarrow1,
       \\
 \Prob\!\left\{n^{5/3-\epsilon}\le k_{\rm mix}
                   \le e^{\epsilon n}\right\}&\longrightarrow1.

\end{align}

For every $b>0$, the preceding good spectra satisfy
\begin{equation}

 \sigma_\Lambda\{|u_1|\le b n^{1/3}\}
 \le C L_n^2 b.
\end{equation}
In particular, given any positive deterministic $b_n\to0$, one can
choose $L_n\to\infty$ with $L_n\le\log n$ and $L_n^2b_n\to0$, so that
the probability in \eqref{eq:spherical-small-ball} tends to zero
uniformly over the resulting good spectra.

Let $W$ be the augmented GOE matrix and let $v$ be a unit top eigenvector.
For every positive deterministic sequence $b_n\to0$,
\begin{equation}
 \mu\{x:|v^Tx|\le b_n n^{1/3}\}
 \longrightarrow0
 \quad\text{in probability}.

\end{equation}
The probability here includes the auxiliary diagonal used to define $v$;
the Gibbs measure itself does not depend on that diagonal.

For every positive deterministic sequence $M_n\to\infty$,
\[
 \Prob\!\left\{
 \frac{n^{2/3}}{M_n}\le\|\Sigma\|\le M_n n^{2/3}
 \right\}\longrightarrow1.
\]

Let $\eta=2d-1$ and
\begin{equation}
 g_X(z)=\frac{p_{N+1}(a_1,\ldots,a_N,z)}{p_N(a_1,\ldots,a_N)}.
\end{equation}
Then $g_X$ is monic of degree $N$ for generic $X$, and
\begin{equation}
 m(X)=\sum_{i=1}^Na_i-\eta[z^{N-1}]g_X(z).
\end{equation}
The right side is understood by continuation at removable
singularities.

There are constants $C,c,d_*>0$ such that, for each fixed $d\ge d_*$,
on events of disorder probability tending to one,
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{x,y}\log\frac{S_d(x,y)}{\mu(y)}
       \le Cn e^{-c d^{2/3}}.
\end{equation}
The assertion holds on one common event for the full system and all
single-site cavities.  The order of the quantifiers is fixed $d$, followed
by $n\to\infty$.

At the comparison times of \Cref{lem:exact-good-times},
\begin{equation}
 q_s\in\mathcal X_v\mathbin{\widehat\otimes}\mathcal S_s.
\end{equation}
The statement concerns the vector factor of the random-coefficient
projection and requires no regularity of its scalar coefficients.

For $0<\xi<\lambda$ and fixed $A,M,H$, on disorder events of
probability tending to one,
\begin{equation}
 \E\norm{p_a(X_a)}^2
 \le C_\xi e^{-2(\lambda-\xi)(b-a)}
             \E\norm{p_b(X_b)}^2+C_\xi n^{-H},
 \qquad 0\le a\le b\le T\le A\log n.

\end{equation}
The estimate is uniform over arbitrary initial laws and all polynomial
tests in the specified envelope.  For every deterministic $u\in\R^n$
with $\norm u=1$, it implies the pointwise envelope
\begin{equation}
 \sup_x\norm{dS_t(u^tx)(x)}^2
 \le C_\xi e^{-2(\lambda-\xi)t}+C_\xi n^{-H},
 \qquad 0\le t\le A\log n.

\end{equation}

For every fixed $0<\beta<1$ and $\epsilon\in(0,1)$, put
$t_n=\log n/(2\lambda)$.  Then
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 \sup_x\norm{S_{(1-\epsilon)t_n}(x,\cdot)-\mu}_{\TV}
       &\longrightarrow1,\\
 \sup_x\norm{S_{(1+\epsilon)t_n}(x,\cdot)-\mu}_{\TV}
       &\longrightarrow0,
 \end{aligned}

\end{equation}
in probability over the disorder.  The number $\lambda>0$ is given
entirely by the dimension-first equilibrium construction in
\Cref{eq:exact-lambda}.

There are constants $d_0,c_{\mathrm a}>0$, independent of $n$ and
the realization in $\mathcal G_n$, such that, for all sufficiently
large $n$,
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{x\in\Omega_n}\P_x(X_u\in\mathcal B)
 \le q_n:=e^{-c_{\mathrm a}\sqrt n}
 \qquad(u\ge d_0).

\end{equation}

There is a constant $C<\infty$ such that, uniformly over
$\mathcal G_n$ and all sufficiently large $n$,
\begin{equation}
 d_c\!\left(\frac14\log n\right)
 \ge1-Cn^{-1/2}.

\end{equation}
The continuous median mixing time
\[
 t_n^{\rm med}=\inf\{t\ge0:d_c(t)\le1/2\}
\]
satisfies $d_c(t_n^{\rm med})=1/2$ and, for some constants $0<c<C<\infty$,
\begin{equation}
 c\log n\le t_n^{\rm med}\le C\log n.

\end{equation}

In the coordinates \eqref{eq:groupoid-coordinates}, the $D$-linear
extension of integration $\epsilon_Y:A_Y\to\one$ is the map
\begin{equation}
 (x_\alpha)_{[\alpha]}\longmapsto
       \sum_{[\alpha]}\frac{x_\alpha}{a_\alpha}.
\end{equation}
For a map $f:Y\to Z$, its pushforward has coordinate formula
\begin{equation}
 \bigl(f_!(x)\bigr)_\beta
   =\sum_{[\alpha]:[f\alpha]=[\beta]}
       \frac{a_\beta}{a_\alpha}\,x_\alpha.
\end{equation}
Consequently $\chi_\beta(f_!1)$ is the rational scalar
\begin{equation}
 \left|
   \mathcal X_\Gamma(Y)
       \mathop{\times}^{h}_{\mathcal X_\Gamma(Z)}\{\beta\}
 \right|_{\mathrm h}
\end{equation}
times the unit of $D$.

For every open subgroup $U\le_o\Gamma$, every finite continuous $U$-set
$X$, and every $h\geq0$,
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{\substack{V\le_o U\\{}[U:V]=p^h}}
       \bigl(|X^V|-|X|\bigr)
       \in p^{h+1}\Z_{(p)}.
\end{equation}
The sum is over all open subgroups of the indicated index.

For a finite \(p\)-group \(P\) and integers \(k,l\ge0\),
\begin{equation}
 T(k)\wedge U_P(L_{T(l)}S)\ne0
 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad k+\ell(P,1)\le l.
\end{equation}

For every finite \(p\)-group \(P\) and all \(m,k\ge0\),
\begin{equation}
 m<k+\ell(P,1)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
 T(k)\wedge U_P(L_m^fS)=0.
\end{equation}

There are positive constants $c_1,c_2,C$ and $n_0$ such that, for every
regular honeycomb cylinder with $n\ge n_0$ columns and every $u\ge0$,
