The macro contains an ordinary plumbing of the three spheres
\begin{equation}
 X_1=-S_j,\quad U,\quad X_2=S_{j+1},\qquad
 X_1^2=X_2^2=-4,\quad UX_1=UX_2=1,\quad X_1X_2=0.

\end{equation}
In particular \(b^+(P)=1\) and \(\sigma(P)=-4\).

The framed ASD and spinor corrections supplied by this end are
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\text{state}&\text{charge congruence}&\delta_I&\delta_{\rm sp}\\ \hline
 +,-&\kappa_N\in\Z&8\kappa_N&6\kappa_N\\
 \text{trace}&\kappa_N\in\frac14+\Z
 &2+8(\kappa_N-\frac14)&2+6(\kappa_N-\frac14)
\end{array}
\end{equation}
For a split main field there are reference fillings with
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
\text{state}&d=v(S)&\kappa_{\rm ref}&\text{choice for an active line}\\ \hline
 +&0&0&K(S)=\Lambda S\\
 -&\pm4&1&|\Lambda S+d|\le4\\
 \text{trace}&\pm2&\frac14&\text{the prescribed boundary eigenline}
\end{array}
\end{equation}
Here $|\Lambda S|=2$.  With these choices the pure-line tangential correction,
including stabilizer matching, is zero.

Let $f_D$ be centered, root-invariant, finite-radius vectors on
the $(D+1)$-regular tree.  Root invariance means that the vector
itself is fixed by every rooted permutation of complete child
subtrees, including their seed coordinates, at every vertex of
its finite neighborhood.  Suppose their occupation embeddings
converge strongly to $v$ at a fixed finite height and their
expected top-generation occupations are bounded uniformly in
$D$.  Place the same rooted vector at the endpoints of an edge,
calling the resulting vectors $f_{D,i}$ and $f_{D,j}$.  Then
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{D\to\infty}\frac{\sqrt D}{2}
       \braket{f_{D,i}}{f_{D,j}}
       =\cE(v):=\re\braket{v}{\cL v}.
\end{equation}
For any centered $v,w$,
\begin{equation}
 |\cE(v)-\cE(w)|
       \leq(\|v\|+\|w\|)\|v-w\|.
\end{equation}

abstract:
For every finite-dimensional complex vector space <i>V</i>, we prove that the canonical Foulkes–Howe map \(\mathop{\mathrm{Sym}}
olimits ^b(\mathop{\mathrm{Sym}}
olimits ^a V)\longrightarrow\mathop{\mathrm{Sym}}
olimits ^a(\mathop{\mathrm{Sym}}
olimits ^b V)\) is surjective whenever <i>a</i> ≥ 2 and \(b\ge a(a-1)\). This gives a quadratic stabilization bound independent of \(\dim V\).

statements.text:
Fix a mixed Sobolev comparison order $k\ge10$. For every sufficiently
small fixed $\delta_2>0$, one may fix $\varepsilon_0>0$ and then
$\nu_0>0$ sufficiently small as follows. Suppose the exact
reduced-equation Cauchy jets on $s=0$ satisfy the vacuum constraints
and wave-map gauge, agree with $g$ for $v<(1-\nu_0)T$, and differ
from the jets of $g+h^p$ by at most
\begin{equation}
 e^{-7\kappa T/2}
 \quad\hbox{in }H^{k+1}\times H^k(dx\,d\theta).

\end{equation}
Then these data have an exact vacuum development $g'$ on
\eqref{pk2:domain}, with normalized error
$F=g'-g-h^p$ bounded in the through-$k$ mixed energies by
\begin{equation}
 C e^{(\kappa+\delta_2)T}
 \left(e^{-7\kappa T/2}+A_T\lambda_p^{-M_1}
                         +A_T^2\lambda_p^{P_*}\right).
 \tag{P7}
\end{equation}
The conclusion holds when this bound has a fixed exponential margin
below $A_T$ and the required base packet norms are sufficiently
exponentially small. The same estimate, with fixed buffers, controls
normalized $C^2$ error.

For every fixed base-order buffer $N_b$, every prescribed inverse
accuracy $M_1$, and sufficiently high finite phase and amplitude
orders, there is a real exterior perturbation $h^B$ such that
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 \|h^B\|_{C^{N_b}}&\le A_T\lambda_p^{N_b+3/2}e^{o(T)},\\
 \text{reduced-wave and gauge residuals}
   &\le e^{o(T)}
       \left(A_T\lambda_p^{-M_1}+A_T^2\lambda_p^{P_0}\right),
 \qquad P_0=2N_b+7,
 \end{split}
 \tag{E2}
\end{equation}
after enlarging $N_b$ for the required trace and derivative buffers.
Its jets match those of $h^p$ at $\mathcal S$ with linear error
$A_T\lambda_p^{-M_1}e^{o(T)}$. The positive exponent $P_0$ depends
only on the base orders, not on later formal accuracy. On the
original initial slice its support lies in
\eqref{pk2:arrival-shell}, up to arbitrarily thin tube margins.

Assume that each equation has the splitting \eqref{fe:F13}, and that its correction, raw, remainder, and mixed budgets satisfy, in the triangular order specified below, bounds of the form
\begin{equation}
o_{R_0\to\infty}(1)(\mathsf F+\mathsf S_a)
  +C\sum_{\rm prior}\mathsf F_{\rm prior}
  +C(R_0)\mathsf K_R+C_{\rm fix}(e_{\rm in}+\mathsf N^{1+c_1})
\end{equation}
for fixed \(c_1>0\). Assume also that the corrections have initial weighted energy and averaged
starting-annulus costs bounded by the initial and overlap terms in
\eqref{fe:source-hypothesis}; the annular norm is the compact weighted
$L^2$ norm. Then the two energy multipliers give
\begin{equation}
\mathsf F\ \le\ o(1)(\mathsf F+\mathsf S_a)
          +C(R_0)\mathsf K_R+C_{\rm fix}(e_{\rm in}+\mathsf N^{1+c_2})
\end{equation}
(with \(c_2>0\), allowing analogous small input bootstrap errors). It suffices also to take tunably small uniform coefficients on \(\mathsf F\) in this argument. A convenient prior order for linear terms is: level \(P\) before the \(h\) hierarchies, within a level lower total orders first, more lab times first at fixed total order, and then fewer \(S\)'s first. Small radius gains and perturbative nonlinear effects may cross this order. Norms aggregated at equal indices are treated together.

With integer parameters in both infima,
\[
 \lim_{k\to\infty}
 \inf_{\substack{r\in\Z\\r\ge2}}
 \frac{\log W_r(k)}{k\log r}=\infty,
 \qquad
 \lim_{r\to\infty}
 \inf_{\substack{k\in\Z\\k\ge3}}
 \frac{\log W_r(k)}{\log r}=\infty.
\]
In particular, $W_r(k)^{1/k}\to\infty$ for every fixed integer
$r\ge2$, and $W_r(k)/r^A\to\infty$ as $r\to\infty$ for every fixed
integer $k\ge3$ and every fixed real $A>0$.

For every $a>0$, every bounded continuous real function $f$ on $\R^3$,
and every $\eps>0$, there is $\rho_0(a,f,\eps)>0$ with the following
property. Fix $0<\rho<\rho_0$ and put $\eta=\rho a^3$. For every
sequence $N_j,L_j\longrightarrow\infty$ with
$N_j/L_j^3\longrightarrow\rho$,
\begin{equation}
 \limsup_{j\to\infty}\ \sup_{\Gamma\in\mathcal G_{N_j,L_j,a}}
 \left|
 \frac{1}{N_j\sqrt\eta}
 \sum_{\substack{k\in(2\pi/L_j)\Z^3\\k\ne0}}
 f\!\left(\frac{k}{\sqrt{8\pi\rho a}}\right)n_{\Gamma,L_j}(k)
       -\int_{\R^3}f\,\dd\nu_{\mathrm{Bog}}
 \right|\le\eps.
\end{equation}

For all sufficiently large $D$, uniformly in \eqref{set:scales},
\begin{equation}
 E_N-E_{N-m}\le C\alpha m\qquad(0\le m\le N).
\end{equation}
At fixed $K,r$, the ratio $E_n/n$ is nondecreasing among positive
particle numbers having nonempty form domain.

One can fix $\alpha_0$ sufficiently large and then $\ell_*>0$
sufficiently small so that every normalized nonnegative bosonic ground
function satisfies, for every $A\subset\mathcal L$,
\begin{equation}
 \mu\{n_{B_v}<\ell_*D\text{ for every }v\in A\}
           \le (C/D^2)^{|A|}.
\end{equation}
The estimate holds for all sufficiently large $D$, uniformly in $K$
and the ground function.

For sufficiently small $x>0$, depending on the previously chosen
exponent $u$, the measures above satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \frac1{|\mathcal C_d|}\sum_{v\sim w}
     \left\{\int|\ell_v-1|^4\,\dd P_{\mathrm{aug},v}
          +\int|\ell_w-1|^4\,\dd P_{\mathrm{aug},w}\right\}
       \le C_jD^{-1-u/8}.
\end{equation}
The summation can be restricted to adjacent cells in the same $R$-cell.
The bound is uniform in volume and in the nonnegative ground function,
with the limiting convention of Section~\ref{set:section}.

For sufficiently small $x>0$ relative to the previously fixed exponents,
the functions $s_v$ of Lemma~\ref{palm:reference} satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \frac1{|\mathcal A_{K,R}|}\sum_{(v,w)\in\mathcal A_{K,R}}
       \int |s_v(Y')-s_w(Y')|^2\,\dd Y'
       =o(D^{-1})+o_K(1).
\end{equation}
The $o(D^{-1})$ is uniform over the nonnegative ground vectors and over the
fixed window of $\alpha$.  The thermodynamic error is taken to zero first.

For some $c_4>0$, the left augmented likelihood satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \int\ell_{v,w}^2\,\dd P_{{\rm aug},v}
       \le \E[T_v^{\rm rel}(T_w^{\rm rel})^2]
                           +CD^{-1-c_4}+o_K(1)
\end{equation}
after averaging over $\mathcal A_{K,R}$.  Its mass satisfies
\eqref{long:mass-bound}, and the same estimates hold with $v,w$ exchanged.

Put $Q_R=P_R-P_0$. Uniformly over normalized, possibly complex,
bosonic ground vectors,
\begin{equation}
 D\|(Q_R)_1\Phi\|_2^2\le16\epsilon_j+o(1)+o_K(1),
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon_j\to0$. The coefficient $16$ is independent of $j$;
the dilute remainder may depend on $j$. The exponents $u,w$ and the scale $R$ may be
kept fixed when $j$ is increased; all dilute and volume thresholds may
depend on $j$.

The constants in the preceding definitions can be chosen so that
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
 \Pp(z\text{ is bad})&\le C_xD^{-1-1/8},\\
 \Pp(\text{every site in }A\text{ is bad})&\le p^{|A|}
                 &&(A\subset\cL),\\
 \Pp(\text{full-path bounds fail in a fixed neighborhood})
                  &\le C_xD^{-6}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
In the second line $p>0$ may be any sufficiently small fixed number,
after increasing the threshold for $D$.  The constants and thresholds
are uniform in the position of the neighborhood and in the volume.

For each fixed integer $k\ge3$ there are constants
$C_k,c_k,\varepsilon_k>0$ such that, for every $N\ge2$,
\begin{equation}
 r_k(N)\le C_kN\exp\bigl(-c_k(\log N)^{\varepsilon_k}\bigr).
\end{equation}
Equivalently, there is $A_k\ge1$ such that an $\alpha$-dense subset
of $[N]$ contains a nonconstant $k$-term progression whenever
\begin{equation}
 \log N\ge A_k\bigl(2+\log(1/\alpha)\bigr)^{A_k},
 \qquad 0<\alpha\le1.
\end{equation}

Choose the vectors of \Cref{prop:small-vectors}.  Their affine
coordinates in the fixed frame satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \ha{z(t),a,P}\le C(h+\log d).
\end{equation}
There are center neighborhoods at every place, with a single integral
description outside a fixed finite set of rational primes, such that
for any selected weighted set $\mathcal V$ on the actual center branches,
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{v\in\mathcal V}w_v
       \log\max(1,\norm{z(t),P}_v)
       \le C(1+\log h+\log d).
\end{equation}
These statements hold for the finitely many conjugate fixed charts.
They also hold after a bounded denominator and a polarized isogeny
of any one fixed multiplier are applied to the vectors.

For fixed $A,\kappa>0$, the set of QM points $t\in S(\Qbar)$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
 h(t)\le A d(t)^\kappa
\end{equation}
is finite.

For every $T,R<\infty$ and $\eps>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{y\downarrow0}\limsup_{n\to\infty}
 \P\left(\sup_{0\le t\le\tau_n(T,R)}
 |H_n(t,y)-\eta_n(t)|>\eps\right)=0.
\end{equation}

For every bounded even local observable $f$, the limit
\begin{equation}
 Q(f)=\kappa^{-1}\lim_{|x|\to\infty}|x|^2\E_0[fB_x]

\end{equation}
exists.  It defines a linear functional that kills constants, is invariant
under lattice translations and square symmetries, and satisfies $Q(b_e)=1$.
Pure transfer preserves $Q$ exactly.  If $f$ is supported in $B_s(x)$,
then
\begin{equation}
 |Q(f)|\leq Cs\|f\|_\infty.

\end{equation}
For centered $f$ and $R\geq4s$,
\begin{equation}
 \|P_{R,x}f\|_\infty\leq C(s/R)\|f\|_\infty.

\end{equation}
If $Q(f)=0$, the factor $s/R$ improves to $(s/R)^u$; it improves to
$(s/R)^w$ if, after auxiliary integration, $f$ is also invariant under
quarter turns about $x$.  More generally, with $v=u$ or $v=w$ according
to these two symmetry classes,
\begin{equation}
 \|P_{R,x}f-Q(f)P_{R,x}B_x\|_\infty
      \leq C(s/R)^v\|f\|_\infty.

\end{equation}
All constants are independent of $s,R$, and $f$.

One can choose $L$ large, then $b>0$ small, and finally $x_*>0$ such that
for every input field of Section~\ref{sec:local-map} with $X\le x_*$,
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 |m'-Lm|&\le C_LX^2,\\
 Y'&\le\rho Y+C_L(|m|+X^2),\\
 X'&\le\rho X+C_L(\sqrt W+Y),
 \end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\rho<1$ and all constants are independent of the scale $s$.
The constants denoted by $C_L$ may depend on all the fixed step
parameters, including the subsequently chosen $b$.

Let a compact set $K_0$ have fixed positive clearance from the boundary
of a finite domain, and fix a sufficiently small physical radius $R>0$.
At the tuned couplings, uniformly in boundary spin assignments,
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{r\downarrow0}\limsup_{\delta\downarrow0}
 \E_{\rm env}\mu_\omega\bigl(
   \text{some }z\in K_0\text{ has four passages from }r\text{ to }R
                              \bigr)=0.

\end{equation}
Passages are counted with the parameter-interval convention above.

For the local arrays just specified, the scale-step parameters can be
chosen in the order \(L\) sufficiently large, then \(b>0\)
sufficiently small, then \(x_*>0\).  There are constants
\(0<\lambda<1\) and \(C_1<\infty\), independent of the spacing, such
that every input with \(X\le x_*\) satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \begin{split}
 |m'-Lm|&\le C_1X^2,\\
 Y'&\le\lambda Y+C_1(|m|+X^2),\\
 X'&\le\lambda X+C_1(\sqrt W+Y).
 \end{split}

\end{equation}
Primes denote the output coordinates, with charge normalization
\(q_{Ls}\).  For every fixed \(H>R_0\),
\begin{equation}
 |v_H-W|\le C_HX^3\qquad (X\le1).

\end{equation}
Furthermore, fix finite constants \(A,B\).  For every sufficiently
large fixed \(H\), whenever \(0<z\le1\), \(X\le Az\), and
\(Y\le Bz^2\), one has
\begin{equation}
 v'_H-v_H=-2\kappa W^2
       \sum_{y\in\Z^2:\,H<|y|\le LH}|y|^{-2}
       +e_H z^4+O_H(z^5).

\end{equation}
Here \(|e_H|\) is bounded by a deterministic quantity tending to zero
as \(H\to\infty\).  The constants may depend on \(A,B\) and the fixed
step parameters, but are uniform in the lattice spacing.

The physical temperatures in Proposition~\ref{prop:two-trajectories}
coincide:
\begin{equation}
             \beta_*:=\beta_{\rm p}=\beta_{\rm d}.

\end{equation}
Thus both the primal and the exact dual coupling arrays at $\beta_*$
have uniformly small scale trajectories with $X_k\to0$.

Let $H(\gamma)$ be the span of cell row indices on the cylinder of period $Nh$. For every $\delta>0$, fixed $p\ge0$ and $K>0$, for both winding and contractible polygons,
\begin{equation}
                   \sum_{\gamma/v,\ H(\gamma)\ge N^{1+\delta}}
                         (1+|\gamma|)^p w(\gamma)=O(N^{-K}) .                 
\end{equation}

For this finite-width cylinder,
\begin{equation}
 \mathscr H=
 \sum_{\text{cylinder polygons using both marked edges}}
                              x_*^{|\gamma|}.                         
\end{equation}
The sum includes both contractible and winding polygons, with each
unoriented polygon counted once.

Let \(h\) be a minimal homeomorphism of an infinite compact metrizable
space \(X\). Then
\begin{equation}

\rc\bigl(C(X)\rtimes_h\Z\bigr)=\frac12\mdim(X,h)
\qquad\text{in }[0,\infty].
\end{equation}

Let \(h\) be a minimal homeomorphism of an infinite compact metrizable
space \(X\), and put \(A=C(X)\rtimes_h\Z\). The following conditions are
equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \(\mdim(X,h)=0\);
\item \((X,h)\) has the small boundary property;
\item \(A\cong A\otimes\mathcal Z\);
\item \(\dim_{\mathrm{nuc}}(A)<\infty\);
\item \(\dim_{\mathrm{nuc}}(A)\le1\).
\end{enumerate}
In particular, if \(\mdim(X,h)\in(0,\infty]\), then
\(A\not\cong A\otimes\mathcal Z\) and
\(\dim_{\mathrm{nuc}}(A)=\infty\).

For every \(X,G,D,\ell\) as above, the natural addition maps
\begin{equation}
 \bigoplus_{\mathcal O}C_{D,\mathcal O,\mathbb Q}
       \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }C_{D,\mathbb Q},
 \qquad
 \bigoplus_{\mathcal O}C_{D,\mathcal O,\ell}
       \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }C_{D,\ell}
\end{equation}
are isomorphisms. The sums range over the nilpotent orbits of \(\Gd\).

Let $u$ satisfy the chamber, divisibility, degree-zero, and genericity
conditions above.  Let $v$ be any fixed regular positive tuple in
$\Lambda_0^r$, without a parity restriction.  For all sufficiently
large integers $n$,
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{[\mathcal E]}(T_{nu+v})_{\mathcal E,\mathcal E}
   =\operatorname{Tr}_{\mathrm{alt}}
           (\mathrm{Fr}^{n}T_v\mid M(u)).
\end{equation}
The diagonal sum uses ordinary matrix entries on the rational
isomorphism classes of bundles with level, and is finite.  The assertion
retains arbitrary multiplicities in $D$ and the allowed central quotient.

For every effective divisor $D$, the rational cuspidal space has the
decomposition
\begin{equation}
             C_{D,\mathbb Q}
                 =\bigoplus_{\mathcal O}C_{D,\mathcal O,\mathbb Q}
\end{equation}
with the spectral-support summands specified in the introduction.

Let $(M_n,A_n)$ have law \eqref{eq:fk-law}, and let $a_n>0$ be any
deterministic sequence with $a_n\to0$ such that
\begin{equation}
 (V_n,a_nd_n,\mu_n)\ \Longrightarrow\ (S,D_h,\mu_h)
\end{equation}
in Gromov--Hausdorff--Prokhorov law. Given the map, start $r$ independent
simple random walks $X^{n,1},\ldots,X^{n,r}$ independently from $\mu_n$.
Given $h$, start $r$ independent Liouville Brownian motions
$B^{h,1},\ldots,B^{h,r}$ independently from $\mu_h$, with the convention
\eqref{eq:continuum-form}. For every fixed integer $r\ge1$,
\begin{equation}
 \left(\overline M_n,a_nd_n,\mu_n;
       (\overline X^{n,j}_{nt})_{t\ge0,\,1\le j\le r}\right)
 \ \Longrightarrow\
 \left(S,D_h,\mu_h;
       (B^{h,j}_t)_{t\ge0,\,1\le j\le r}\right)
\end{equation}
in the curve-decorated Gromov--Hausdorff--Prokhorov topology.

Under Lemma~\ref{lem:frames}, let $0\le q\le n$ and let $\pi_q$ be uniform on ordered
distinct $q$-tuples of positions. For every fixed ordered list $C$
of $q$ distinct labels,
\begin{equation}
 \|\mathcal L(Z_T(C))-\pi_q\|_{\TV}
 =
 \|\mathcal L(X^{\boldsymbol\iota}_T(L_0^{-1}C))-\pi_q\|_{\TV}.
\end{equation}
Thus the same-list distances agree when $L_0=I$. Their averages
also agree when $C$ is sampled uniformly over ordered distinct lists,
independently of the switch process:
\begin{equation}
 \E_C\|\mathcal L(Z_T(C)\mid C)-\pi_q\|_{\TV}
 =
 \E_C\|\mathcal L(X^{\boldsymbol\iota}_T(C)\mid C)-\pi_q\|_{\TV}.
\end{equation}

There is a deterministic labeled partition
$\mathcal M=(M_1,\ldots,M_k)$ into blocks of size $m$ for which
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{a=1}^k\delta_a(\mathcal M)
 \le k\varepsilon_n=o(1)\qquad(d\longrightarrow\infty).
\end{equation}

Let a finite set \(X\) be partitioned into \(k\ge1\) nonempty sets
\(M_1,\ldots,M_k\), and put \(G=\Sym(X)\). Let \(H_a\) be the subgroup
permuting \(M_a\) and fixing \(X\setminus M_a\) pointwise. Suppose that a
probability measure \(\nu\) on \(G\) satisfies, for some \(B\ge 1\),
\begin{equation}

  \nu(gH_a)\le B\frac{|H_a|}{|G|}
  \qquad(g\in G,\ 1\le a\le k).
\end{equation}
Then every irreducible complex unitary representation \(\rho\) of \(G\), of
dimension \(D\), satisfies
\begin{equation}

  \|\widehat\nu(\rho)\|_{\op}^2
  \le \frac{B}{D}\sum_{a=1}^k
       \sum_{\substack{\pi\in\widehat H_a\\\dim\pi\le D^{1/k}}}
                    (\dim\pi)^2.
\end{equation}
The block sizes need not be equal.

Let $d\ge3$, $q=7N/8$, and $T=1024d$. For a uniformly sampled
ordered distinct input tuple $C$, independent of $X$,
\begin{equation}
 \E_C\|\mathcal L(X_T(C)\mid C)-\pi_q\|_{\TV}
 \le\frac{q\sqrt N}{2}
 \left(e^{-16d}+2(N+1)e^{-N/256}\right)^{1/2}=o(1).
\end{equation}
The corresponding hidden-card energy is $O(N^{-20})$,
uniformly over its index.

Fix integers $T\ge1$ and $1\le q\le n$. Let
$\mathbf v=(v_1,\ldots,v_T)$ be a random schedule of nonzero directions.
Set $Z_0=I$, and suppose that, conditional on $\mathbf v$, the
increments of $Z$ are independent uniform switches in directions
$v_1,\ldots,v_T$. Let $C=(c_1,\ldots,c_q)$ be a deterministic list of
distinct labels, or a random such list independent of the joint process
$(\mathbf v,(Z_t)_{t=0}^T)$. For $1\le j\le q$, put
\begin{align}
 \mathcal F_t^{C,j}
 &=\sigma\bigl(C,v_1,\ldots,v_t,\,
       Z_s(c_i):0\le s\le t,\ i<j\bigr), \\
 \mathcal H^{C,j}
 &=\sigma\bigl(C,\mathbf v,\,
       Z_s(c_i):0\le s\le T,\ i<j\bigr). 
\end{align}
For $0\le t\le T$, define
$p_t(x)=\Pr\{Z_t(c_j)=x\mid\mathcal F_t^{C,j}\}$.
Then, for every $x\in V$,
\begin{equation}
 \Pr\{Z_t(c_j)=x\mid\mathcal H^{C,j}\}=p_t(x)
 \quad\text{almost surely}. 
\end{equation}
For $t<T$, let
$K_t(v)=\Pr\{v_{t+1}=v\mid v_1,\ldots,v_t\}$. Then
\begin{equation}
 \Pr\{Z_t(c_j)=x,\ v_{t+1}=v\mid\mathcal F_t^{C,j}\}
 =p_t(x)K_t(v). 
\end{equation}

Let $d\ge4$. Put $b=16$, $m=n/b$, $q=n-m$, and
$T=(2+20b)d=322d$.
For every fixed ordered list of $q$ distinct input positions, its image
under the physical shuffle at time $T$ has distance at most
\begin{equation}
 \delta_d=\frac{4T}{m}+\frac12n^{3/2}
                (1-1/(4b))^{(T-2d+1)/2}=o(1)

\end{equation}
from a uniform injection, uniformly over the chosen list.

There is an absolute $C$ such that for every irreducible unitary
representation $\rho$ of $G$, of degree $D$, and unit vectors $u,z$,
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb P\{\mathcal G_{\mathrm{ent}},
       |\langle z,\rho(g_T)u\rangle|\ge D^{-1/8}\}
 \le C D^{-1/(2L)}.

\end{equation}
Put $a=\mathbb P(\mathcal G_{\mathrm{ent}})$. When $a>0$, the conditional endpoint law
$\eta=\mathcal L(g_T\mid\mathcal G_{\mathrm{ent}})$ is defined and satisfies
\[
 \|\widehat\eta(\rho)\|_{\rm op}
 \le D^{-1/8}+\frac{C}{a}D^{-1/(2L)}.
\]
For all sufficiently large $d$, Lemma~\ref{frames:typical-history}
gives $a\ge1/2$. In that range the last bound is at most
$C'D^{-1/(2L)}$ for the absolute constant $C'=1+2C$.

Let $C=(a_1,\ldots,a_k)$ be a uniformly chosen ordered list of distinct
initial cards, independent of $X_T\sim\mu$, and let $\pi_k$ be uniform
on ordered $k$-tuples of distinct positions. Then
\begin{align}
 \mathbb E_C\bigl\|\mathcal L(X_T(C)\mid C)-\pi_k\bigr\|_{\rm TV}
 &\le\varepsilon_n,\notag\\
 \varepsilon_n
 &=\frac{k}{2}\sqrt{n\bigl(e^{-25d}+T\delta_n\bigr)}=o(1).

\end{align}

There is an absolute $P\ge2$ such that for every dyadic $n\ge2$,
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{Tr}_{\rm reg}|Q_n|^P\le1+n^{-10}.
\end{equation}
If $v$ is an integer with $2v\ge P$, then $v$ independent sweeps have total-variation distance at most $\frac12n^{-5}$ from uniform, from every deterministic initial deck. Here $\|\mu-\nu\|_{\rm TV}=\tfrac12\sum_g|\mu(g)-\nu(g)|$.

Let $n=2^d$, $d\ge0$, and let $Q_n$ be a sweep in any fixed order of the distinct coordinates. For either $B_n=Q_n^*Q_n$ or $B_n=Q_nQ_n^*$, there are an absolute integer $u\ge1$ and $C<\infty$ such that
\begin{equation}
 B_n^u(x,y)\le\frac{e^{Cl}}{(n)_l},\qquad 0\le l\le n,

\end{equation}
for every pair of ordered injections $x,y$ of $l$ distinct cards. Here $(n)_l=n!/(n-l)!$, including $(n)_0=1$. In particular,
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{Tr}_{\rm reg}|Q_n|^{2u}\le e^{Cn}.

\end{equation}

There are absolute constants $M,P_s$ such that, for all sufficiently large dyadic $n$,
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{\substack{\lambda\vdash n\\1\le n-\lambda_1\le n e^{-M\sqrt{\log n}}}}
 D_\lambda\operatorname{Tr}_{V_\lambda}|Q_n(\lambda)|^{P_s}
 \le n^{-12}.
\end{equation}

Fix \(A_0,A_1>0\).  For all sufficiently large \(n\), if
\begin{equation}
 ne^{-A_0\sqrt{\log n}}\le X:=\log D_\lambda\le A_1n,

\end{equation}
then every pair of projections just described satisfies
\begin{equation}
 D_\lambda\operatorname{Tr}_{V_\lambda}(E_RE_F)
 \le D_RD_F\exp\!\left(\frac{X}{(\log n)^{1/3}}\right).

\end{equation}

For every $f,g\in\cA_{\C}$ there is a unique finite complex Borel measure
$\nu_{f,g}$ on $B$ such that, for every finite-dimensional representation
$D$ of $\Ghat$,
\begin{equation}
 [T_Df,g]=\int_B\chi_D\,d\nu_{f,g}.
\end{equation}
For every closed invariant subset $Y\subset\Nhat$,
\begin{equation}
 f\in\cF_Y\cA_{\C}
 \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
 \operatorname{supp}(\nu_{f,g})\subset B_Y
 \quad\text{for every }g\in\cA_{\C}.
\end{equation}

Assume~\(s\geq2\).  A line~\(H\subset A_F\) is the inertia of a
quasi-divisorial valuation if and only if
\begin{equation}
 H=S_1\cap S_2
 \quad\text{for alternating subspaces }S_1,S_2\subset A_F
 \text{ of dimension }s.

\end{equation}
The quasi-divisorial valuation~\(v\) with~\(I_v=H\) is unique.  For any nonzero
\(h\in H\), its decomposition space is
\begin{equation}
 D_v=\{g\in A_F:(h,g)\text{ is alternating}\}.

\end{equation}

There is an integer $G_X\ge0$, depending only on the embedded model $X$,
with the following property.  Given $g_1,\ldots,g_m\in L^\times$ whose
classes in $V_L$ are independent, there is a quasi-prime
$(d-1)$-divisor $w$ of $L/l$ such that:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $lw$ is algebraically closed and algebraic over a finite field of
characteristic different from $\ell$, and $Lw$ is the function field of a
smooth projective curve $C_w/lw$ of genus at most $G_X$;
\item the $g_j$ are $w$-units and their residue classes are independent
in $V_{Lw}$;
\item for every $j$,
\[
 \#\{x\in C_w(lw):\ord_x(\overline{g_j})\not\equiv0\pmod\ell\}
       =s_X([g_j]).
\]
\end{enumerate}

Suppose $\psi_0$ is a normalized product state and its trajectory under
$W$ is regular.  Let $P_0=|\psi_0\rangle\langle\psi_0|$ be the full
product projection and $H_0$ its count.  For any product test $B$ at the
output and every fixed $0<p<h<1$,
\begin{equation}
 \norm{\one_{H_0\ge m^h}W^{\dagger}P_BW\psi_0}
 \le \exp(-m^p)
 \quad\text{eventually}.
\end{equation}

Under the hypotheses of Corollary~\ref{cor:product-tail}, write
$\psi_t=W\psi_0$.  For any product test $B$ at the output and every
fixed $0<p<h<1$,
\begin{equation}
 \norm{P_B\psi_t}^{2}
 \norm{\one_{H_B\ge m^h}\psi_t}
 \le \exp(-m^p)
 \quad\text{eventually}.
\end{equation}
For each fixed $(h,p)$, both this estimate and
\eqref{eq:product-tail} hold eventually uniformly over the output test
$B$.

For every sufficiently small fixed $\eta>0$, the joint critical mass,
including all actual count choices in the bins, is at most
\begin{equation}
 h^{C_1\eta(m+1)}\prod_i\frac{\kappa_i^2}{K_i}
                    \prod_{ij\text{ tested}}s_{ij}^{-1}
\end{equation}
for $h$ sufficiently large depending on $\eta$.  Constant factors per
node are included in this bound.

At each sufficiently large-count node, the proof leaves an independent
group of at least $c k_i$ increments available for a concentration test.
An interval of at most $a_i$ allowed total heights contributes
\begin{equation}
                    C\min(1,a_i\kappa_i^{-4/3}),
\end{equation}
provided the interval is measurable outside that group, with any fixed
external data allowed.  A proved concentration bound for another
additive statistic, such as length, may be used on this group instead.
A different independent group supplies
\begin{equation}
                         C e^{-c k_i q(l)}
\end{equation}
when the node's total length is at most $l$.

Several height factors may be used for a specified vector of heights and
then summed over an explicitly bounded admissible set, or in a successive
integration order with the stated measurability property.  The estimate
does not condition on the reserved group's own horizontal displacement.
Bounded-count nodes have the trivial bounded-factor version of the height
estimate.  One-row paths have $K_i=\kappa_i=1$ and bounded mass, with no
atom or independent length test asserted.

For each sufficiently small $\epsilon>0$ there are $c_\epsilon>0$ and
$n_\epsilon$ such that, for $n\ge n_\epsilon$,
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{L(\gamma)=n,\ D(\gamma)\ge n^{3/4+\epsilon}}
                  \rho^{L(\gamma)}\le e^{-n^{c_\epsilon}}.
\end{equation}
More precisely, uniformly for $d\ge n^{3/4+\epsilon}$,
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{L(\gamma)=n,\ D(\gamma)\ge d}\rho^{L(\gamma)}
                  \le \exp(-c_\epsilon d/n^{3/4}).
\end{equation}
Walks are rooted at one fixed vertex, or counted in the translation
convention of Proposition~\ref{R:geo:absolute-count}.  These are
absolute masses before division by any finite or thermal partition.

For every fixed sufficiently small $\xi>0$ there are $c,C>0$ such that the unnormalized critical mass of all plane self-avoiding walks rooted at a fixed center obeys
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{R\le {\rm diam}\,\omega\le 2R,\ |\omega|\le R^{d_*-\xi}}\mu(\omega)
       \ \le\ C\exp(-R^c).                                                   
\end{equation}

Let \(V_R\) denote the mass of all center walks from a fixed center with diameter at most \(R\). We have for every \(\nu>0\)
\begin{equation}
 V_R\le \exp(C_\nu(1+R)^\nu).                                                
\end{equation}

For each $C<\infty$ and $\eta>0$ there are $c>0$, $M_0<\infty$ and $H_0<\infty$ such that, for every integer $H\ge H_0$ and relative-port target $y=(H,x)\in\Gamma$ with $|x|\le CH$,
\begin{equation}
 \sum_{\lceil K\rceil\le k\le\lfloor2K\rfloor}
 p^{\otimes k}\left\{
 \sum_{i=1}^kY_i=y,\quad
 \sum_{i=1}^kL_i\le H^{4/3+\eta},\quad
 \sum_{i=1}^k\Delta_i\le M_0H
 \right\}
 \ge c K H^{-2}=cH^{-5/4}.

\end{equation}
Incompatible counts contribute zero. In particular the paths counted here remain within distance $M_0H$ of their initial port.

Under $\mathbb P_T^{\mathrm{th}}$, as $T\to\infty$,
\[
 \begin{gathered}
 \ell(\gamma)=T^{1+o(1)},\qquad
 \operatorname{diam}(\gamma)=T^{3/4+o(1)},\\
 |\gamma_{\ell(\gamma)}-\gamma_0|=T^{3/4+o(1)}
 \end{gathered}
\]
in probability. Each statement means convergence within every fixed
positive exponent slack.

If \(V\) is finite and \(H(V)\le d^2\) in the preceding actual
experiment, then
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal J(V)\le I_{\mathrm{act}}(S;V\mid D,DS)+Cd.
\end{equation}

Fix $0<\gamma<1/2$. Let $\mu_j$ satisfy
\eqref{eq:prop-upper}--\eqref{eq:prop-riesz} with the same constants,
and suppose that $0\in\supp\mu_j$. Let $\delta_j>0$ and
$K_j\in\mathbb N$ satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \delta_j\to0,\qquad K_j\to\infty,\qquad
 \delta_j2^{\gamma K_j}\to0,\qquad
 \delta_j^{-1}2^{-K_j}\to0.
\end{equation}
Assume that $2^{K_j}\le\diam(\supp\mu_j)$ and
\begin{equation}
 e_{\mu_j}(0,2^\ell)\le\delta_j2^{\gamma\ell}
       \qquad(0\le\ell\le K_j).
\end{equation}
Put $A_j=8\,2^{K_j}$. Suppose, for every compactly supported
1-Lipschitz scalar function $\varphi$ with
$\supp\varphi\subset B(0,A_j)$ and $\int\varphi\,d\mu_j=0$, that
\begin{equation}
 |\mathcal R_{\mu_j}(\varphi)|\le\delta_j^3.
\end{equation}
There is one subsequence along which, for every fixed $r>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{e_{\mu_j}(0,r)}{\delta_j}\longrightarrow0,
 \qquad b_{\mu_j}(0,r)\longrightarrow0.
\end{equation}

For each integer \(m\ge2\) and \(M<\infty\), take \(r_0=m+15\).
There are constants \(K,N\), independent of \(0\le\sigma\le1\),
such that, whenever \(\|u\|_{r_0,\sigma}\le M\) and \(|n|\ge N\),
the derivative of \Cref{ana:constraint-definition} has the form
\begin{equation}
(\dd C_n)_u(v)=[b_n(u)v]_n.
\end{equation}
Here
\begin{equation}
\|b_n(u)-1\|_{m,(1-K/n^2)\sigma}\le\frac{K}{|n|},
\end{equation}
and, for two such inputs on the same strip,
\begin{equation}
\|b_n(u)-b_n(\widetilde u)\|_{m,(1-K/n^2)\sigma}
\le\frac{K}{|n|}\|u-\widetilde u\|_{r_0,\sigma}.
\end{equation}
The derivative identity holds for smooth real variations, and for
real-symmetric holomorphic variations with the indicated finite norms.

Unless the boundary is a circle, there is a single-valued holomorphic
function \(\mathfrak s\) on the joint tube
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal T=\left\{(y,t):t\text{ is in the parameter disk},\quad
 -h_X(\bar t)<\Im y<h_X(t)\right\}

\end{equation}
such that
\begin{equation}
 X(y,t)=\Gamma(\mathfrak s(y,t)),\qquad
 |\Im\mathfrak s(y,t)|<H.

\end{equation}
It satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \mathfrak s(y,0)=y,\qquad
 \mathfrak s(y+2\pi,t)=\mathfrak s(y,t)+2\pi,\qquad
 \mathfrak s(y,-t)=\mathfrak s(y,t).

\end{equation}
It also obeys real symmetry,
\(\mathfrak s(\bar y,\bar t)=\overline{\mathfrak s(y,t)}\).
After shrinking the parameter disk, for every real \(t\) the real
map \(y\mapsto\mathfrak s(y,t)\) descends to an orientation-preserving
analytic circle diffeomorphism. The inequality in
\eqref{shape:global-composition} is void when \(H=\infty\).

Let \(N\) be an envelope as in \Cref{critical:def:envelope}, assume
the first-moment bound \eqref{critical:eq:first-moment}, and suppose
\(\sup_{k\ge k_0}\mu(k)/N(k)<\infty\). Then
\begin{equation}
 \mu(k)\le C N(k),\qquad
 \E\frac{d_k|P|}{N(k)^2}\le C.
\end{equation}
There is \(\eta>0\), depending only on the fixed envelope constants,
such that
\begin{equation}
 \left\|\frac{d_kM}{N(k)}\right\|_{2+\eta}\le C.
\end{equation}
The estimates for \(M\) hold also with suprema on every fixed tile.
If \(N\) is nondecreasing, then in addition
\begin{equation}
 \sup_x d_kM\le C(1+k)N(k),\qquad
 \sup_x\frac{d_k|P|}{N(k)^2}\le C(1+k)^2.
\end{equation}

Let \(N\) satisfy the hypotheses of \Cref{thm:critical-bootstrap},
with fixed constants, and let \(d_a=e^{-k_a}\) be a sufficiently
small ancestor depth. Let \(\omega,\nu\) be as above, with fixed
kernel comparison constants. Suppose that for every fixed tile
enlargement needed below,
\begin{equation}
 \left\|\sup_{s\in\mathcal T_A(j,x)}d_jM(s)\right\|_{L^2(\nu)}
 \le C_A N(j),\qquad k_0\le j\le k_a+3,
\end{equation}
and that
\begin{equation}
 \int |P(x+\ii(H-d_a))|\,\dd\omega(x)
 \le C\frac{N(k_a)^2}{d_a}.
\end{equation}
Then on all finer levels, and with every fixed tile enlargement,
\begin{equation}
 \left\|\sup_{s\in\mathcal T_A(j,x)}d_jM(s)\right\|_{L^2(\nu)}
 \le C'_A N(j).
\end{equation}
The constants are independent of \(k_a\), of \(\omega\), and of the
finite bootstrap ratio. No higher weighted moment is asserted.

Assume that no sequence in \Cref{def:prohibited-sequence} exists.
Then there are \(C>0\) and \(\eps_1\in(0,1)\) such that
\begin{equation}
 \mu_f(k)\le C(1+k)^{1-\eps_1}\qquad(k\ge k_0).
\end{equation}

If no sequence in \Cref{def:prohibited-sequence} exists, then for
some \(\eps>0\) and \(C<\infty\),
\begin{equation}
 \int_{k_0}^K\mu_f(k)^2\,\dd k\le C(1+K)^{2-\eps}
 \qquad(K\ge k_0).
\end{equation}
In particular \Cref{thm:sequence-exclusion} will establish this
conclusion and \Cref{cor:critical-polynomial-bounds} in the critical
case.

For every compact horizontal interval \(I\subset\R\) and every
\(\eps>0\), there is a constant \(C_{I,\eps}\) such that
\begin{equation}

 |F(x-\ii D)|\le C_{I,\eps}D^{-\lambda-\eps}
 \qquad(x\in I,\ 0<D<1).
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{equation}

 \sup_{x\in I}\log|F(x-\ii e^{-v})|
       \le\lambda v+o(v)\qquad(v\to\infty),
\end{equation}
where the error is uniform on \(I\).

For every sufficiently large \(K\), there is a deterministic
\(k=k(K)\in[0.8K,2.2K]\) such that, on each shape row of depth
\(e^{-k}\),
\begin{equation}
 |\log|f||>K^{\alpha/2}
\end{equation}
outside a set of relative horizontal measure tending to zero as
\(K\to\infty\).

Assume that $X$ is a mixing two-sided finite-alphabet stationary process whose
alphabet partition has entropy rate zero. For every centered finite-valued
one-site function $f$ on $X$, every probability coupling $x:Y\to X$, every
family satisfying \eqref{eq:projective-characters}, every automorphism $G$,
and every $l\in L$, one has
\begin{equation}

 \lim_{N\to\infty}\frac1N\sum_{r=0}^{N-1}
 \left|\int f(T_X^r x)\overline{\chi_{G^r l}}\dd\eta\right|=0.
\end{equation}

There are absolute constants $p\in(1,2]$ and $C<\infty$ such that,
for every $N=2^d$, every coordinate order, every $0\le l\le N$,
either choice of $H_N$, and every $x\in\mathcal X_{N,l}$,
\begin{equation}
 \frac1{(N)_l}\sum_{y\in\mathcal X_{N,l}}
       \big((N)_lH_N(x,y)\big)^p\le e^{Cl}.
\end{equation}
After increasing $C$, the same constants give
\begin{equation}
 N^{-l}\sum_{y\in\{0,1\}^{dl}}
       \big(N^lH_N(x,y)\big)^p\le e^{Cl},
\end{equation}
where the kernel is extended by zero on tuples with repeated positions.
Both conclusions hold with rows replaced by columns.

There are an absolute integer $u\ge1$ and an absolute $C<\infty$
such that, for every $N=2^d$, every coordinate order, every
$0\le l\le N$, every $x,y\in\mathcal X_{N,l}$, and either
$H_N=T_N^*T_N$ or $H_N=T_NT_N^*$,
\begin{equation}
 H_N^u(x,y)\le\frac{e^{Cl}}{(N)_l}.
\end{equation}
In particular, with unnormalized regular trace on $S_N$,
\begin{equation}
 \operatorname{Tr}_{\mathrm{reg}}|T_N|^{2u}\le e^{CN}.
\end{equation}

There are absolute constants $c,C,\zeta>0$, a number $\delta\in(0,1)$,
and an integer $p_0$ with the following properties.  For every dyadic $n$
and $\lambda=(n-k,\beta)\vdash n$ with $k\ge1$,
\begin{align}
 W_n(\lambda)&\le e^{-c\Lambda_n(k)},\\
 W_n(\lambda)&\le e^{C\Lambda_n(k)}f_\beta^{-\zeta}.

\end{align}
Independently, for every $1\le k\le n$, let $X_{n,k}$ be the ordered injective $k$-tuples of positions, with
uniform probability measure, and let
$\mathcal K_{n,k}(x,y)=(n)_k\Pr_{K_n}(x\mapsto y)$ be its kernel density.
Then
\begin{align}
 \sup_x\E_y\mathcal K_{n,k}(x,y)^{1+\delta}
   &\le e^{C\Lambda_n(k)},\\
 \operatorname{tr}_{X_{n,k}}K_n^{p_0}
   &\le e^{C\Lambda_n(k)}.
\end{align}
Consequently, after changing absolute constants,
\begin{align}
 \|T_n|_{V_\lambda}\|_{\op}
   &\le \exp[-c\{\Lambda_n(k)+\log f_\beta\}],
       \\
 W_n(\lambda)&\le e^{-c_1\Lambda_n(k)}D_\lambda^{-c_2}

\end{align}
for absolute $c_1,c_2>0$.

For any fixed input tuple of $k$ distinct positions and any law $Q$
on the raw switches, the actual tracked cycle counts satisfy
\begin{equation}
 \E_Q\sum_j C_j^*\le C\Lambda_n(k)+C D(Q\Vert\mathrm{fair}),
\end{equation}
with an absolute constant $C$.

There is an absolute $c_1>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large dyadic
$n$ and all surviving nontrivial $\lambda\vdash n$ with
$k\ge n^{1/100}$,
\begin{equation}
 \max\left\{\|T_nP_{\rm lo}^D|_{V_\lambda}\|,
              \|P_{\rm lo}^CT_n|_{V_\lambda}\|\right\}
 \le2^{-c_1F_\lambda}.
\end{equation}
The two projections refer to different product subgroups; no commutation
between them is asserted.

There is an absolute real exponent $8\le p<\infty$ such that, for every $d\ge1$ and every $\lambda\vdash n$ with $n=2^d$,
\begin{equation}
 f^\lambda \|(\mu_d)_\lambda\|_p^p \ \le\ 1\qquad (\lambda\vdash n).

\end{equation}

There is an absolute $p_*\ge1$ such that, for every dyadic $n$ and
every irreducible type $\lambda\vdash n$,
\begin{equation}
 D_\lambda\Tr\!\left(((B_n^*B_n)|_{V_\lambda})^{p_*}\right)\le1.
\end{equation}
Consequently $\|B_n|_{V_\lambda}\|\le D_\lambda^{-1/(2p_*)}$. The moment in the display is the Schatten power $2p_*$, because $\operatorname{Tr}(B_n^*B_n)^{p_*}=\|B_n\|_{2p_*}^{2p_*}$.

For every \(J>0\) and finite-range real potential \(U\) satisfying the
symmetries of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, the preceding scale parameters can
be fixed independently of all domains and boundary data.  The initial
scale \(s_0\) may be enlarged by any prescribed finite amount before the
following construction.  For each sufficiently small \(\epsilon>0\)
there is \(\lambda_*>0\) and a real analytic function
\(\beta_c:(-\lambda_*,\lambda_*)\to(0,\infty)\), with
\(\beta_c(0)=\beta_0\), for which the initial potential
\eqref{eq:flow-physical-input} has a trajectory
\(V_{k+1}=\Phi_{s_k}(V_k)\) satisfying
\begin{equation}
 \|V_k\|_{s_k,d}\le\epsilon2^{-k},\qquad k\ge0.
\end{equation}
Its intermediate one-step trajectories obey
\begin{equation}
 \sup_{0\le t\le1}
 \sum_\ell |\ell|e^{(d/2)(|\ell|-1)}
 \|v_{k,\ell}(t)\|_\infty\le C\epsilon2^{-k}.
\end{equation}
The same construction applies to both signs of \(\lambda\).

The quadratic moment $\kappa$ of the physical bounded-profile
generators satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \mathbf1_{(s,\infty)}(\kappa)
 \mathbf1_{[0,s]}(h_0)\big|_l=0\qquad(s\geq0).
\end{equation}

Put $\ell=l-S$.  Its insertion at imaginary infinity annihilates every
compact physical vacuum polynomial:
\begin{equation}
 \lim_{R\to\infty}R^4\ip{\ell(iRe)}{A\Omega}=0.
\end{equation}

The formal scalar vacuum wave equals the accessible scalar:
\begin{equation}
 l=S,\qquad \ell=0.

\end{equation}

Let $\gamma_0=\pi/4$. In the notation
\eqref{physical:marker}--\eqref{physical:marker-sum}, let $B_{\rm out}$
and $B_{\rm in}$ be fixed finite products of $\xi$-twist and
$\zeta$-twist rows, respectively, at real arguments in $[-v,v]$.
Suppose that each product includes at least $n\ge1$ rows at arguments
in $\{-\gamma_0,\gamma_0\}$. Uniformly for $x,y\in[-v,v]$,
\begin{align}
 \limsup_{\substack{N\to\infty\\N\ \mathrm{even}}}
 \|B_{\rm out}\mathcal N_N^{\mathrm{stag}}(x)B_{\rm in}\|_{G_N}
       &\le C_\lambda n^{-1+\rho},\\
 \limsup_{\substack{N\to\infty\\N\ \mathrm{even}}}
 \|B_{\rm out}\bigl(\mathcal N_N^{\mathrm{stag}}(x)
       -e^{\ii\tau(x-y)}\mathcal N_N^{\mathrm{stag}}(y)\bigr)
       B_{\rm in}\|_{G_N}
       &\le C_\lambda n^{-1-\rho}.

\end{align}
Immediately on either side of either marker sum one may insert a product
of an even number of the corresponding twist rows, at arbitrary real
arguments in $[-v,v]$. The products may differ between the two terms;
the constant may depend on a common bound for these additional counts.
Every finite setup is fixed before the limit in $N$ is taken.

Fix an integer \(n\ge1\) and, at a fixed mesh, a finite physical-layer
setup as above.  All layer counts and all inserted edge matrices are fixed
before even \(N\to\infty\).  Let \(B_{\mathrm{out},N}\) and
\(B_{\mathrm{in},N}\) be products of the corresponding \(\xi\)- and
\(\zeta\)-twist rows in \eqref{physical:vacuum-rows}, each containing at
least \(n\) rows.  Every row is at a physical angle \(\pm\gamma_0\).

For \(x,y\in\{-\gamma_0,\gamma_0\}\), define either
\begin{align}
 \mathcal D_N^{\mathrm{low}}
 &=E_{\mathrm{out},N}\widehat{\mathcal N}_N(x)E_{\mathrm{in},N},
 \\
 \mathcal D_N^{\mathrm{high}}
 &=E^x_{\mathrm{out},N}\widehat{\mathcal N}_N(x)E^x_{\mathrm{in},N}
 -e^{\ii\tau(x-y)}E^y_{\mathrm{out},N}\widehat{\mathcal N}_N(y)
       E^y_{\mathrm{in},N}.

\end{align}
Each \(E\) is a separately even product of at most a fixed \(L\) physical
rows with the corresponding twist.  The lists for the two high terms may
differ, but the exterior networks in \eqref{physical:external-states}
are common to them.  Every row in these displays has its own divisor
\(\Lambda_N\).  Set, for \(a=\mathrm{low},\mathrm{high}\),
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal C_N^a=\frac1{|\mathcal S_N|}\sum_{\sigma\in\mathcal S_N}
 \left\langle\psi_{\mathrm{out},\sigma,N},
 B_{\mathrm{out},N}\mathcal D_N^a B_{\mathrm{in},N}
 \psi_{\mathrm{in},\sigma,N}\right\rangle_{G_N}.
\end{equation}
Then \(\mathcal C^a=\lim_{N\to\infty,\ N\ \mathrm{even}}\mathcal C_N^a\)
and \(R_{\mathrm{in}},R_{\mathrm{out}}\), the corresponding limits of
\eqref{physical:external-states}, exist.  They are the square-plane
scalar coefficient and its finite nonnegative reflected vacuum ratios.
They satisfy
\begin{align}
 |\mathcal C^{\mathrm{low}}|
 &\le C_{\lambda,L}n^{-1+\rho}
       \sqrt{R_{\mathrm{out}}R_{\mathrm{in}}},
 \\
 |\mathcal C^{\mathrm{high}}|
 &\le C_{\lambda,L}n^{-1-\rho}
       \sqrt{R_{\mathrm{out}}R_{\mathrm{in}}}.

\end{align}
The analogous fixed-diagram reflection across an ordinary tile-axis cut
gives the plane Cauchy--Schwarz inequality with its two reflected ratios.

For the source tensor there are numbers $a_n(R_0,L)$ independent
of both probes, with $c_L\le|a_n|\le C_L$, such that
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathcal H_f(z,u)&=a_n(R_0,L)h_n(z)J_n(u)+e_n(z,u;R_0,L),\\
 \lim_{R_0\to\infty}\limsup_{n\to\infty}
       \sup_{(z,u)\in K}|e_n|&=0
 \end{aligned}

\end{equation}
for each compact probe set $K$, with sufficiently large fixed $L$.
Every locally uniform subsequential limit of $J_n$ on the reserved
